window.EPH_TOPIC_DATA.topics.push({"id":"def-bank-mathematics","title":"Practice Bank — Mathematics (100 MCQs)","subject":"Mathematics (NDA)","tag":"100 questions · answers with explanations","blurb":"100 exam-style mathematics MCQs. Pick an option on any question to see the answer and explanation instantly.","intro":"100 exam-style multiple-choice questions on Mathematics, written for the NDA / CDS / AFCAT / Agniveer written papers. Tap an option to check your answer — the correct answer and a short explanation appear immediately. Everything runs in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere and nothing is saved between visits.","sections":[{"h":"How to use this bank","body":"<p>Work through the questions in timed batches (aim for roughly one question per minute), then read every explanation — including the ones you got right. Patterns repeat across defence papers: the same concept keeps returning in new clothes.</p>"}],"questions":[{"q":"If A = {x : x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0}, then the number of elements in A is","options":["1","3","0","2"],"answer":3,"expl":"x^2 - 5x + 6 = (x-2)(x-3) = 0 gives x = 2, 3, so A has 2 elements."},{"q":"The value of (1 + i)/(1 - i) is","options":["1 + i","-i","i","1"],"answer":2,"expl":"Multiply numerator and denominator by (1 + i): (1+i)^2/2 = 2i/2 = i."},{"q":"The discriminant of the quadratic equation x^2 + 4x + 4 = 0 is","options":["16","-16","8","0"],"answer":3,"expl":"D = b^2 - 4ac = 16 - 16 = 0, so the equation has equal real roots."},{"q":"If |A| = 5 for a 3x3 matrix A, then |2A| equals","options":["10","15","40","30"],"answer":2,"expl":"For an n x n matrix, |kA| = k^n |A|; here 2^3 x 5 = 40."},{"q":"The value of sin 75 degrees is","options":["(sqrt(6) - sqrt(2))/4","(sqrt(3) + 1)/4","sqrt(3)/2","(sqrt(6) + sqrt(2))/4"],"answer":3,"expl":"sin 75 = sin(45+30) = sin45 cos30 + cos45 sin30 = (sqrt(6)+sqrt(2))/4."},{"q":"The limit of (sin x)/x as x tends to 0 is","options":["0","does not exist","infinity","1"],"answer":3,"expl":"This is the standard limit lim(x->0) sin x / x = 1."},{"q":"If f(x) = x^3, then f'(x) equals","options":["3x","x^2","3x^2","x^3/3"],"answer":2,"expl":"By the power rule, d/dx(x^n) = n x^(n-1), so d/dx(x^3) = 3x^2."},{"q":"The integral of x^2 dx is","options":["2x + C","3x^2 + C","x^3/3 + C","x^3 + C"],"answer":2,"expl":"Integrate termwise: integral of x^n dx = x^(n+1)/(n+1) + C, giving x^3/3 + C."},{"q":"The mean of 2, 4, 6, 8 and 10 is","options":["6","7","5","8"],"answer":0,"expl":"Mean = (2+4+6+8+10)/5 = 30/5 = 6."},{"q":"The probability of getting a head when a fair coin is tossed once is","options":["1/4","1","1/2","1/3"],"answer":2,"expl":"A fair coin has two equally likely outcomes, so P(head) = 1/2."},{"q":"If f(x) = x^2, then f'(2) equals","options":["8","2","0","4"],"answer":3,"expl":"f'(x) = 2x, so f'(2) = 4."},{"q":"The value of log base 10 of 100 is","options":["20","1","10","2"],"answer":3,"expl":"10^2 = 100, so log10(100) = 2."},{"q":"The sum of the first 10 natural numbers is","options":["45","50","55","100"],"answer":2,"expl":"Sum = n(n+1)/2 = 10 x 11/2 = 55."},{"q":"The 7th term of the A.P. 2, 5, 8, ... is","options":["17","26","23","20"],"answer":3,"expl":"a = 2, d = 3; 7th term = a + 6d = 2 + 18 = 20."},{"q":"The 4th term of the G.P. 3, 9, 27, ... is","options":["81","54","72","108"],"answer":0,"expl":"Common ratio r = 3; 4th term = a r^3 = 3 x 27 = 81."},{"q":"If x + 1/x = 2, then x^2 + 1/x^2 equals","options":["6","2","0","4"],"answer":1,"expl":"Squaring: x^2 + 2 + 1/x^2 = 4, so x^2 + 1/x^2 = 2."},{"q":"(a + b)^2 - (a - b)^2 simplifies to","options":["2ab","0","a^2 + b^2","4ab"],"answer":3,"expl":"Expanding gives (a^2+2ab+b^2) - (a^2-2ab+b^2) = 4ab."},{"q":"If sin theta = 3/5 with theta acute, then cos theta equals","options":["3/4","5/3","4/5","5/4"],"answer":2,"expl":"cos theta = sqrt(1 - sin^2 theta) = sqrt(1 - 9/25) = 4/5 in the first quadrant."},{"q":"The value of tan 45 degrees is","options":["infinity","sqrt(3)","0","1"],"answer":3,"expl":"tan 45 = sin45/cos45 = 1."},{"q":"sin^2 theta + cos^2 theta equals","options":["1","0","2","tan^2 theta"],"answer":0,"expl":"This is the fundamental Pythagorean trigonometric identity."},{"q":"The square root of 144 is","options":["12","16","24","14"],"answer":0,"expl":"12 x 12 = 144, so sqrt(144) = 12."},{"q":"15 percent of 200 is","options":["30","20","35","25"],"answer":0,"expl":"15/100 x 200 = 30."},{"q":"A number increased by 20% and then decreased by 20% results in a net","options":["4% increase","8% decrease","no change","4% decrease"],"answer":3,"expl":"Net change = x - y - xy/100 = 20 - 20 - 4 = -4%, i.e. a 4% decrease."},{"q":"An article bought for Rs 500 and sold for Rs 600 gives a profit of","options":["25%","10%","20%","16.67%"],"answer":2,"expl":"Profit = 100; profit% = 100/500 x 100 = 20%."},{"q":"The simple interest on Rs 1000 at 10% per annum for 2 years is","options":["Rs 210","Rs 200","Rs 100","Rs 121"],"answer":1,"expl":"SI = P x R x T / 100 = 1000 x 10 x 2 / 100 = Rs 200."},{"q":"Rs 1000 invested at 10% compound interest per annum becomes after 2 years","options":["Rs 1200","Rs 1300","Rs 1210","Rs 1100"],"answer":2,"expl":"Amount = 1000 x (1.1)^2 = 1000 x 1.21 = Rs 1210."},{"q":"Two numbers in the ratio 2:3 add up to 50. The numbers are","options":["25 and 25","10 and 40","15 and 35","20 and 30"],"answer":3,"expl":"2x + 3x = 50 gives x = 10, so the numbers are 20 and 30."},{"q":"The average of the first five prime numbers is","options":["5","7","5.6","6"],"answer":2,"expl":"(2+3+5+7+11)/5 = 28/5 = 5.6."},{"q":"The HCF of 12 and 18 is","options":["36","3","6","9"],"answer":2,"expl":"Common factors of 12 and 18 are 1, 2, 3, 6; the highest is 6."},{"q":"The LCM of 12 and 18 is","options":["36","72","18","6"],"answer":0,"expl":"Multiples: 12 -> 12, 24, 36; 18 -> 18, 36; the least common is 36."},{"q":"If x:y = 3:4 and y = 20, then x equals","options":["16","15","10","12"],"answer":1,"expl":"x/20 = 3/4 gives x = 15."},{"q":"A 100 m long train running at 20 m/s crosses a pole in","options":["5 seconds","2 seconds","10 seconds","4 seconds"],"answer":0,"expl":"Time = distance/speed = 100/20 = 5 seconds."},{"q":"A can do a job in 10 days and B in 15 days. Together they finish it in","options":["6 days","5 days","7.5 days","12 days"],"answer":0,"expl":"Combined rate = 1/10 + 1/15 = 1/6 per day, so 6 days."},{"q":"A pipe fills a tank in 6 hours and another empties it in 8 hours. With both open, the tank fills in","options":["14 hours","48 hours","24 hours","7 hours"],"answer":2,"expl":"Net rate = 1/6 - 1/8 = 1/24 per hour, so 24 hours."},{"q":"The determinant of the matrix [[1, 2], [3, 4]] is","options":["2","10","-2","-10"],"answer":2,"expl":"Determinant = (1)(4) - (2)(3) = 4 - 6 = -2."},{"q":"A square matrix has an inverse if and only if its determinant is","options":["zero","1","positive","non-zero"],"answer":3,"expl":"A matrix is invertible exactly when its determinant is non-zero."},{"q":"Two straight lines are parallel when their slopes are","options":["reciprocals","equal","negative reciprocals","zero"],"answer":1,"expl":"Parallel lines make the same angle with the x-axis, so their slopes are equal."},{"q":"The distance between the points (0, 0) and (3, 4) is","options":["5","12","7","25"],"answer":0,"expl":"Distance = sqrt(3^2 + 4^2) = sqrt(25) = 5."},{"q":"The midpoint of the segment joining (2, 4) and (6, 8) is","options":["(3, 5)","(4, 6)","(8, 12)","(4, 4)"],"answer":1,"expl":"Midpoint = ((2+6)/2, (4+8)/2) = (4, 6)."},{"q":"The slope of the line through (1, 2) and (3, 8) is","options":["6","2","4","3"],"answer":3,"expl":"Slope = (8-2)/(3-1) = 6/2 = 3."},{"q":"The equation of the x-axis is","options":["x + y = 0","x = 0","y = 0","x = y"],"answer":2,"expl":"Every point on the x-axis has y-coordinate 0."},{"q":"The circle x^2 + y^2 = 25 has radius","options":["10","25","5","sqrt(5)"],"answer":2,"expl":"Comparing with x^2 + y^2 = r^2 gives r^2 = 25, so r = 5."},{"q":"The derivative of sin x with respect to x is","options":["tan x","-cos x","-sin x","cos x"],"answer":3,"expl":"d/dx(sin x) = cos x is a standard derivative."},{"q":"The derivative of e^x with respect to x is","options":["e","e^x","1","x e^(x-1)"],"answer":1,"expl":"The exponential function is its own derivative: d/dx(e^x) = e^x."},{"q":"The integral of 2x dx is","options":["2x^2 + C","x^2 + C","2 + C","x + C"],"answer":1,"expl":"Integral of 2x = 2 x^2/2 + C = x^2 + C."},{"q":"The area under the curve y = x from x = 0 to x = 2 is","options":["1","2","8","4"],"answer":1,"expl":"Area = integral from 0 to 2 of x dx = [x^2/2] = 2."},{"q":"The order of the differential equation d^2y/dx^2 + 3 dy/dx + 2y = 0 is","options":["0","1","3","2"],"answer":3,"expl":"Order is the highest derivative present, which is the second derivative."},{"q":"The magnitude of the vector (3, 4) is","options":["7","25","5","12"],"answer":2,"expl":"Magnitude = sqrt(3^2 + 4^2) = sqrt(25) = 5."},{"q":"The dot product of two perpendicular vectors is","options":["their product of magnitudes","0","1","-1"],"answer":1,"expl":"a . b = |a||b| cos 90 = 0 for perpendicular vectors."},{"q":"In vector algebra, i cross j equals","options":["-k","0","1","k"],"answer":3,"expl":"By the right-hand rule for unit vectors, i x j = k."},{"q":"If P(A) = 0.3, then P(not A) equals","options":["0","0.3","1.3","0.7"],"answer":3,"expl":"P(not A) = 1 - P(A) = 1 - 0.3 = 0.7."},{"q":"When two dice are thrown, the probability that the sum is 7 equals","options":["1/12","1/36","7/36","1/6"],"answer":3,"expl":"Six favourable pairs (1,6)...(6,1) out of 36 outcomes give 6/36 = 1/6."},{"q":"The median of 3, 7, 5, 9, 1 is","options":["5","9","7","3"],"answer":0,"expl":"Arranged in order: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9; the middle value is 5."},{"q":"The mode of the data 2, 3, 3, 4, 5 is","options":["3","4","5","2"],"answer":0,"expl":"Mode is the most frequent value; 3 occurs twice, more than any other."},{"q":"The standard deviation of the constant data set 5, 5, 5, 5 is","options":["2.5","0","1","5"],"answer":1,"expl":"All values equal the mean, so every deviation is 0 and the SD is 0."},{"q":"sin(90 degrees - theta) equals","options":["cos theta","tan theta","sin theta","-cos theta"],"answer":0,"expl":"This is the co-function identity: sin(90 - theta) = cos theta."},{"q":"The value of cos 60 degrees is","options":["1/2","sqrt(3)/2","1","0"],"answer":0,"expl":"cos 60 = 1/2 is a standard trigonometric value."},{"q":"tan theta can be written as","options":["cos theta / sin theta","1/sin theta","sin theta x cos theta","sin theta / cos theta"],"answer":3,"expl":"By definition tan theta = sin theta / cos theta."},{"q":"If cosec theta = 2, then the acute angle theta equals","options":["45 degrees","90 degrees","60 degrees","30 degrees"],"answer":3,"expl":"cosec theta = 1/sin theta = 2 gives sin theta = 1/2, so theta = 30 degrees."},{"q":"The height of an equilateral triangle of side a is","options":["a","(sqrt(3)/2) a","sqrt(3) a","a/2"],"answer":1,"expl":"Height = sqrt(a^2 - (a/2)^2) = (sqrt(3)/2) a."},{"q":"The area of a triangle with base 10 cm and height 6 cm is","options":["60 sq cm","16 sq cm","36 sq cm","30 sq cm"],"answer":3,"expl":"Area = 1/2 x base x height = 1/2 x 10 x 6 = 30 sq cm."},{"q":"The circumference of a circle of radius 7 cm is","options":["88 cm","22 cm","44 cm","154 cm"],"answer":2,"expl":"Circumference = 2 pi r = 2 x 22/7 x 7 = 44 cm."},{"q":"The area of a circle of radius 7 cm is","options":["44 sq cm","308 sq cm","154 sq cm","88 sq cm"],"answer":2,"expl":"Area = pi r^2 = 22/7 x 49 = 154 sq cm."},{"q":"The volume of a cube of side 3 cm is","options":["27 cubic cm","9 cubic cm","54 cubic cm","18 cubic cm"],"answer":0,"expl":"Volume = side^3 = 3^3 = 27 cubic cm."},{"q":"The total surface area of a cube of side 2 cm is","options":["16 sq cm","24 sq cm","12 sq cm","8 sq cm"],"answer":1,"expl":"Surface area = 6 x side^2 = 6 x 4 = 24 sq cm."},{"q":"The volume of a cylinder of radius 2 cm and height 5 cm is","options":["40 pi cubic cm","10 pi cubic cm","20 pi cubic cm","25 pi cubic cm"],"answer":2,"expl":"Volume = pi r^2 h = pi x 4 x 5 = 20 pi cubic cm."},{"q":"The diagonal of a square of side 5 cm is","options":["10 cm","5 cm","5 sqrt(2) cm","25 cm"],"answer":2,"expl":"Diagonal = side x sqrt(2) = 5 sqrt(2) cm by Pythagoras."},{"q":"The sum of the interior angles of a triangle is","options":["270 degrees","360 degrees","90 degrees","180 degrees"],"answer":3,"expl":"The angle sum of any triangle is always 180 degrees."},{"q":"The number of diagonals of a pentagon is","options":["5","7","10","4"],"answer":0,"expl":"Diagonals = n(n-3)/2 = 5 x 2/2 = 5."},{"q":"If f is an even function, then f(-x) equals","options":["-f(x)","f(x)","0","2 f(x)"],"answer":1,"expl":"An even function satisfies f(-x) = f(x) for all x."},{"q":"log(ab) is equal to","options":["(log a)(log b)","log a - log b","log a / log b","log a + log b"],"answer":3,"expl":"This is the product law of logarithms."},{"q":"2^3 multiplied by 2^2 equals","options":["12","16","32","64"],"answer":2,"expl":"2^3 x 2^2 = 2^5 = 32."},{"q":"sqrt(16) + sqrt(9) equals","options":["12","25","7","5"],"answer":2,"expl":"4 + 3 = 7."},{"q":"The solutions of x^2 - 9 = 0 are","options":["x = 9 or x = -9","x = 3 or x = -3","no real solution","x = 3 only"],"answer":1,"expl":"x^2 = 9 gives x = plus or minus 3."},{"q":"(x + 2)(x + 3) expands to","options":["2x^2 + 5x + 6","x^2 + 5x + 6","x^2 + 6x + 5","x^2 + 5x + 5"],"answer":1,"expl":"FOIL: x^2 + 3x + 2x + 6 = x^2 + 5x + 6."},{"q":"The remainder when x^2 + 1 is divided by (x - 1) is","options":["1","-2","0","2"],"answer":3,"expl":"By the remainder theorem, substitute x = 1: 1 + 1 = 2."},{"q":"If alpha and beta are the roots of x^2 - 7x + 12 = 0, then alpha + beta and alpha x beta are","options":["12 and 7","7 and -12","-7 and 12","7 and 12"],"answer":3,"expl":"Sum of roots = -b/a = 7; product = c/a = 12."},{"q":"The number of subsets of a set with 3 elements is","options":["6","3","9","8"],"answer":3,"expl":"A set with n elements has 2^n subsets; 2^3 = 8."},{"q":"The value of 5P2 (permutations of 5 taken 2 at a time) is","options":["20","25","10","15"],"answer":0,"expl":"5P2 = 5 x 4 = 20."},{"q":"The value of 5C2 is","options":["15","10","5","20"],"answer":1,"expl":"5C2 = 5!/(2! 3!) = 10."},{"q":"The value of 5 factorial is","options":["25","120","20","60"],"answer":1,"expl":"5! = 5 x 4 x 3 x 2 x 1 = 120."},{"q":"If A is a subset of B and B is a subset of A, then","options":["A = B","A and B are disjoint","A is a proper subset of B","B is empty"],"answer":0,"expl":"Mutual inclusion means the sets have exactly the same elements."},{"q":"By De Morgan's law, the complement of (A union B) is","options":["A' intersect B'","A intersect B","A' union B'","A union B"],"answer":0,"expl":"De Morgan: (A U B)' = A' cap B'."},{"q":"The value of i^2, where i is the imaginary unit, is","options":["0","i","-1","1"],"answer":2,"expl":"By definition i = sqrt(-1), so i^2 = -1."},{"q":"The modulus of the complex number 3 + 4i is","options":["12","25","5","7"],"answer":2,"expl":"|3+4i| = sqrt(9+16) = sqrt(25) = 5."},{"q":"The conjugate of 2 - 3i is","options":["-2 + 3i","-2 - 3i","2 + 3i","2 - 3i"],"answer":2,"expl":"The conjugate changes the sign of the imaginary part only."},{"q":"On an Argand diagram, the real part of a complex number is plotted on the","options":["y-axis","x-axis","unit circle","origin only"],"answer":1,"expl":"The horizontal axis represents the real part and the vertical axis the imaginary part."},{"q":"If z = 1 + i, then z^2 equals","options":["2","0","1 + 2i","2i"],"answer":3,"expl":"(1+i)^2 = 1 + 2i + i^2 = 1 + 2i - 1 = 2i."},{"q":"The value of e^(i pi) + 1 is","options":["-1","2","0","1"],"answer":2,"expl":"Euler's identity: e^(i pi) = -1, so e^(i pi) + 1 = 0."},{"q":"The slope-intercept form of a straight line is","options":["y = mx","y = mx + c","x = my + c","ax + by + c = 0"],"answer":1,"expl":"y = mx + c gives slope m and y-intercept c directly."},{"q":"Two distinct parallel lines have","options":["two points in common","no point in common","infinitely many points in common","exactly one point in common"],"answer":1,"expl":"Parallel lines never meet, so they share no point."},{"q":"The equation x^2 + y^2 + 2gx + 2fy + c = 0 represents a","options":["straight line","ellipse","parabola","circle"],"answer":3,"expl":"This is the general second-degree form of a circle."},{"q":"The focus of the parabola y^2 = 4ax is at","options":["(0, 0)","(0, a)","(a, 0)","(-a, 0)"],"answer":2,"expl":"For the standard right-opening parabola y^2 = 4ax, the focus is (a, 0)."},{"q":"The eccentricity of an ellipse is","options":["less than 1","greater than 1","always 0","equal to 1"],"answer":0,"expl":"An ellipse has 0 < e < 1; e = 1 gives a parabola and e > 1 a hyperbola."},{"q":"The limit of 1/x as x tends to infinity is","options":["0","does not exist","infinity","1"],"answer":0,"expl":"As x grows without bound, 1/x approaches 0."},{"q":"The second derivative of x^3 with respect to x is","options":["6","6x","3x^2","x^2"],"answer":1,"expl":"First derivative 3x^2; differentiating again gives 6x."},{"q":"At a local maximum or minimum of a differentiable function, f'(x) is","options":["undefined","infinite","1","0"],"answer":3,"expl":"Stationary points occur where the first derivative vanishes."},{"q":"The definite integral from 0 to 1 of x dx equals","options":["1","1/4","2","1/2"],"answer":3,"expl":"Integral = [x^2/2] from 0 to 1 = 1/2."},{"q":"If the roots of ax^2 + bx + c = 0 are equal, then","options":["a = b = c","b^2 > 4ac","b^2 = 4ac","b^2 < 4ac"],"answer":2,"expl":"Equal roots occur exactly when the discriminant b^2 - 4ac is zero."},{"q":"The sum of the infinite G.P. 1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + ... is","options":["3/2","1","infinity","2"],"answer":3,"expl":"Sum = a/(1-r) = 1/(1 - 1/2) = 2, since |r| < 1."}]},
{"id":"def-bank-english","title":"Practice Bank — English (100 MCQs)","subject":"GAT (English) + SSB","tag":"100 questions · answers with explanations","blurb":"100 exam-style english MCQs. Pick an option on any question to see the answer and explanation instantly.","intro":"100 exam-style multiple-choice questions on English, written for the NDA / CDS / AFCAT / Agniveer written papers. Tap an option to check your answer — the correct answer and a short explanation appear immediately. Everything runs in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere and nothing is saved between visits.","sections":[{"h":"How to use this bank","body":"<p>Work through the questions in timed batches (aim for roughly one question per minute), then read every explanation — including the ones you got right. Patterns repeat across defence papers: the same concept keeps returning in new clothes.</p>"}],"questions":[{"q":"Choose the correctly spelt word:","options":["Occurrance","Ocurrence","Occurrence","Occurence"],"answer":2,"expl":"'Occurrence' (double c, double r) is the correct spelling."},{"q":"Choose the correctly spelt word:","options":["Embarass","Embarrass","Embaress","Embarras"],"answer":1,"expl":"'Embarrass' has a double r and double s."},{"q":"Choose the correctly spelt word:","options":["Acommodation","Accommodation","Acomodation","Accomodation"],"answer":1,"expl":"'Accommodation' has double c and double m."},{"q":"Choose the correctly spelt word:","options":["Colonal","Colonel","Kernal","Cornel"],"answer":1,"expl":"'Colonel' (pronounced 'kernel') is the correct spelling of the army rank."},{"q":"Fill in the blank: She has been working here ___ 2019.","options":["for","by","from","since"],"answer":3,"expl":"'Since' is used with a point of time (2019); 'for' is used with a duration."},{"q":"Fill in the blank: He is good ___ mathematics.","options":["on","at","in","for"],"answer":1,"expl":"The fixed preposition with 'good' is 'at' (good at something)."},{"q":"Fill in the blank: The train arrived ___ time.","options":["by","in","on","at"],"answer":2,"expl":"'On time' means punctual; 'in time' means early enough for something."},{"q":"Fill in the blank: She divided the sweets ___ the two children.","options":["with","between","among","into"],"answer":1,"expl":"'Between' is used for two persons or things; 'among' for more than two."},{"q":"Fill in the blank: He has no control ___ his temper.","options":["of","on","at","over"],"answer":3,"expl":"The idiom is 'control over' something."},{"q":"Fill in the blank: I am looking forward ___ meeting you.","options":["for","of","to","at"],"answer":2,"expl":"'Look forward to' is followed by a gerund (meeting), so 'to' is correct."},{"q":"Choose the correct sentence:","options":["Neither of the two boys are present.","Neither of the two boys were present.","Neither of the two boys was present.","Neither of the two boy was present."],"answer":2,"expl":"'Neither' is singular, so it takes the singular verb 'was'."},{"q":"Choose the correct sentence:","options":["Each of the students has a book.","Each of the students has books.","Each of the student has a book.","Each of the students have a book."],"answer":0,"expl":"'Each' is singular and takes a singular verb: 'has'."},{"q":"Choose the correct sentence:","options":["The committee have decided the matter.","The committee has decided the matter.","The committee has decide the matter.","The committee have decides the matter."],"answer":1,"expl":"'Committee' as a single body takes a singular verb: 'has decided'."},{"q":"Identify the error: 'He is one of the players who has scored a century.' The error is in","options":["'one' should be 'a'","'the' should be removed","'a' should be 'the'","'has' should be 'have'"],"answer":3,"expl":"The relative pronoun 'who' refers to 'players' (plural), so the verb must be plural: 'have'."},{"q":"Identify the error: 'Scarcely had he entered than the bell rang.' The error is in","options":["'entered' should be 'enter'","'rang' should be 'rung'","'had' should be 'has'","'than' should be 'when'"],"answer":3,"expl":"The correlative of 'scarcely' is 'when', not 'than' ('no sooner' pairs with 'than')."},{"q":"Identify the error: 'He as well as his friends are going.' The error is in","options":["'as well as' should be 'and'","'going' should be 'go'","'are' should be 'is'","'his' should be 'their'"],"answer":2,"expl":"With 'as well as', the verb agrees with the first subject (He), so 'is' is correct."},{"q":"Change into passive voice: 'She wrote a letter.'","options":["A letter was wrote by her.","A letter was written by her.","A letter is written by her.","A letter has been written by her."],"answer":1,"expl":"Simple past active becomes 'was/were + past participle' in the passive."},{"q":"Change into passive voice: 'They are building a bridge.'","options":["A bridge is built by them.","A bridge has been built by them.","A bridge is being built by them.","A bridge was being built by them."],"answer":2,"expl":"Present continuous active becomes 'is/am/are being + past participle' in the passive."},{"q":"Change into passive voice: 'Who broke the glass?'","options":["Whom was the glass broken by?","By whom is the glass broken?","Who was the glass broken by?","By whom was the glass broken?"],"answer":3,"expl":"Interrogative passive: 'By whom + was/were + subject + past participle'."},{"q":"Change into indirect speech: He said, 'I am tired.'","options":["He says that he was tired.","He said that I was tired.","He said that he was tired.","He said that he is tired."],"answer":2,"expl":"In indirect speech the present tense shifts to past: 'am' becomes 'was'."},{"q":"Change into indirect speech: She said, 'I will come tomorrow.'","options":["She said that I would come the next day.","She said that she would come the next day.","She says that she would come the next day.","She said that she will come tomorrow."],"answer":1,"expl":"'Will' becomes 'would' and 'tomorrow' becomes 'the next day' in indirect speech."},{"q":"The synonym of ABANDON is","options":["forsake","retain","cherish","keep"],"answer":0,"expl":"'Abandon' means to give up completely; 'forsake' is its closest synonym."},{"q":"The synonym of CANDID is","options":["cunning","frank","rude","shy"],"answer":1,"expl":"'Candid' means truthful and straightforward, i.e. frank."},{"q":"The synonym of PRUDENT is","options":["reckless","foolish","wise","hasty"],"answer":2,"expl":"'Prudent' means showing care and good judgement, i.e. wise."},{"q":"The synonym of VIVID is","options":["vague","dull","lively","pale"],"answer":2,"expl":"'Vivid' means bright and full of life; 'lively' matches best."},{"q":"The synonym of TRANQUIL is","options":["noisy","restless","peaceful","stormy"],"answer":2,"expl":"'Tranquil' means calm and peaceful."},{"q":"The antonym of TRANSPARENT is","options":["fragile","opaque","clear","obvious"],"answer":1,"expl":"'Transparent' means see-through; 'opaque' means not letting light through."},{"q":"The antonym of EXPAND is","options":["spread","enlarge","contract","extend"],"answer":2,"expl":"'Expand' means to grow larger; 'contract' means to shrink."},{"q":"The antonym of BRAVE is","options":["cowardly","heroic","bold","daring"],"answer":0,"expl":"'Brave' means courageous; its opposite is 'cowardly'."},{"q":"The antonym of ANCIENT is","options":["aged","antique","old","modern"],"answer":3,"expl":"'Ancient' means very old; its opposite is 'modern'."},{"q":"The antonym of VICTORY is","options":["defeat","triumph","success","conquest"],"answer":0,"expl":"'Victory' means winning; its opposite is 'defeat'."},{"q":"One word for 'a person who speaks many languages' is","options":["orator","linguist only","bilingual only","polyglot"],"answer":3,"expl":"A polyglot knows or uses several languages."},{"q":"One word for 'a place where birds are kept' is","options":["aviary","stable","aquarium","apiary"],"answer":0,"expl":"An aviary houses birds; an apiary keeps bees and an aquarium keeps fish."},{"q":"One word for 'one who can use both hands equally well' is","options":["versatile","dexterous","skilful","ambidextrous"],"answer":3,"expl":"'Ambidextrous' literally means right-handed on both sides."},{"q":"One word for 'a government by the few' is","options":["oligarchy","democracy","anarchy","monarchy"],"answer":0,"expl":"Oligarchy is rule by a small group; 'oligos' means few in Greek."},{"q":"One word for 'fear of heights' is","options":["claustrophobia","acrophobia","hydrophobia","xenophobia"],"answer":1,"expl":"'Acro' (height) + 'phobia' (fear) gives acrophobia."},{"q":"The idiom 'to bite the dust' means","options":["to eat quickly","to fail or be defeated","to work hard","to travel far"],"answer":1,"expl":"'Bite the dust' means to suffer defeat or failure."},{"q":"The idiom 'a blessing in disguise' means","options":["an open secret","a religious ceremony","a hidden benefit","a lucky charm"],"answer":2,"expl":"It describes something that seems bad but turns out to be good."},{"q":"The idiom 'to burn the midnight oil' means","options":["to waste fuel","to cook dinner","to celebrate","to work late into the night"],"answer":3,"expl":"It refers to studying or working late at night."},{"q":"The idiom 'once in a blue moon' means","options":["on a holiday","very rarely","every month","at night"],"answer":1,"expl":"A blue moon is rare, so the phrase means something happening very seldom."},{"q":"The idiom 'to hit the nail on the head' means","options":["to say exactly the right thing","to injure oneself","to miss an opportunity","to work as a carpenter"],"answer":0,"expl":"It means to describe exactly what is causing a situation or problem."},{"q":"The phrasal verb 'call off' means","options":["shout","summon","telephone","cancel"],"answer":3,"expl":"'Call off' means to cancel, as in 'call off the meeting'."},{"q":"The phrasal verb 'put up with' means","options":["tolerate","erect","display","postpone"],"answer":0,"expl":"'Put up with' means to endure or tolerate something unpleasant."},{"q":"The phrasal verb 'look into' means","options":["investigate","enter","admire","ignore"],"answer":0,"expl":"'Look into' means to examine or investigate a matter."},{"q":"The phrasal verb 'give up' means","options":["donate","offer","quit","distribute"],"answer":2,"expl":"'Give up' means to stop trying or to surrender."},{"q":"Fill in the blank with the correct tense: By next year, he ___ his training.","options":["will complete","will have completed","has completed","completes"],"answer":1,"expl":"'By next year' with a future completion needs the future perfect: 'will have completed'."},{"q":"Fill in the blank: If I ___ rich, I would travel the world.","options":["will be","were","am","was being"],"answer":1,"expl":"Unreal conditional uses 'were' for all persons: 'If I were rich'."},{"q":"Fill in the blank: She ___ to school every day.","options":["gone","goes","go","is go"],"answer":1,"expl":"Third-person singular present takes 'goes'."},{"q":"Fill in the blank: They ___ playing since morning.","options":["have been","has been","are","were"],"answer":0,"expl":"An action continuing from the past uses present perfect continuous: 'have been playing'."},{"q":"Choose the correct article: ___ honest man is respected everywhere.","options":["An","The","A","No article"],"answer":0,"expl":"'Honest' begins with a vowel sound (the h is silent), so 'an' is used."},{"q":"Choose the correct article: He is ___ university professor.","options":["a","no article needed","the","an"],"answer":0,"expl":"'University' begins with a consonant sound (yoo), so 'a' is used."},{"q":"Identify the part of speech of the underlined word: 'He runs fast.' (fast)","options":["noun","adverb","verb","adjective"],"answer":1,"expl":"'Fast' modifies the verb 'runs', so it is an adverb here."},{"q":"Choose the correct degree: This is the ___ book I have ever read.","options":["best","better","most better","good"],"answer":0,"expl":"Superlative degree 'best' follows 'the' for the highest degree."},{"q":"Fill in the blank: Hardly had he slept ___ the alarm rang.","options":["then","than","so","when"],"answer":3,"expl":"'Hardly/scarcely' pairs with 'when'; 'no sooner' pairs with 'than'."},{"q":"Change the voice: 'The letter was posted by him.' (active)","options":["He has posted the letter.","He had posted the letter.","He posted the letter.","He posts the letter."],"answer":2,"expl":"Passive past 'was posted' converts to active simple past 'posted'."},{"q":"Choose the correct reported speech: She said, 'Where are you going?'","options":["She asked where was I going.","She said where I am going.","She asked where are you going.","She asked where I was going."],"answer":3,"expl":"Questions become statements in indirect speech with the tense shifted back."},{"q":"The synonym of METICULOUS is","options":["careless","careful","hasty","lazy"],"answer":1,"expl":"'Meticulous' means showing great attention to detail, i.e. careful."},{"q":"The synonym of ADVERSE is","options":["kind","unfavourable","helpful","lucky"],"answer":1,"expl":"'Adverse' means harmful or unfavourable."},{"q":"The synonym of ZEAL is","options":["enthusiasm","laziness","apathy","anger"],"answer":0,"expl":"'Zeal' means great energy or enthusiasm."},{"q":"The antonym of TRANQUIL is","options":["still","quiet","turbulent","calm"],"answer":2,"expl":"The opposite of calm/tranquil is turbulent or disturbed."},{"q":"The antonym of HONESTY is","options":["integrity","sincerity","truth","deceit"],"answer":3,"expl":"'Honesty' means truthfulness; its opposite is 'deceit'."},{"q":"The antonym of GENEROUS is","options":["stingy","noble","liberal","kind"],"answer":0,"expl":"'Generous' means giving freely; 'stingy' means unwilling to give."},{"q":"One word for 'a person who eats too much' is","options":["gourmet","vegan","ascetic","glutton"],"answer":3,"expl":"A glutton habitually eats excessively."},{"q":"One word for 'that which cannot be read' is","options":["incredible","inevitable","ineligible","illegible"],"answer":3,"expl":"'Illegible' means handwriting too unclear to read."},{"q":"One word for 'a speech delivered without preparation' is","options":["extempore","elocution","oration","dialogue"],"answer":0,"expl":"'Extempore' means spoken without preparation."},{"q":"The idiom 'to spill the beans' means","options":["to reveal a secret","to waste food","to make a mess","to plant seeds"],"answer":0,"expl":"It means to disclose secret information accidentally or prematurely."},{"q":"The idiom 'a hard nut to crack' refers to","options":["a strong tool","a tasty snack","a difficult problem or person","a broken machine"],"answer":2,"expl":"It describes something difficult to understand or deal with."},{"q":"The phrasal verb 'break down' (of a machine) means","options":["analyse","stop working","demolish","escape"],"answer":1,"expl":"A machine that 'breaks down' stops functioning."},{"q":"The phrasal verb 'take after' means","options":["resemble a parent","remove","chase","undertake"],"answer":0,"expl":"'Take after' means to resemble a family member in looks or behaviour."},{"q":"Fill in the blank: The news ___ shocking.","options":["is","were","have been","are"],"answer":0,"expl":"'News' is singular (uncountable), so it takes 'is'."},{"q":"Fill in the blank: One of my friends ___ coming today.","options":["have","are","were","is"],"answer":3,"expl":"'One' is the subject and is singular, so 'is coming' is correct."},{"q":"Choose the correct preposition: He jumped ___ the river.","options":["into","on","at","in"],"answer":0,"expl":"'Into' shows motion towards the inside of the river."},{"q":"Choose the correct preposition: She is married ___ my cousin.","options":["with","to","for","by"],"answer":1,"expl":"The fixed usage is 'married to' someone."},{"q":"Read the passage and answer: 'Discipline is the bridge between goals and accomplishment. Soldiers who train daily build not only muscle but the habit of showing up when motivation fades.' According to the passage, discipline is described as","options":["a type of physical exercise","the opposite of motivation","the bridge between goals and accomplishment","a military punishment"],"answer":2,"expl":"The passage states this definition in its very first sentence."},{"q":"According to the same passage, daily training builds","options":["only physical strength","muscle and the habit of showing up","motivation alone","punishment for failure"],"answer":1,"expl":"The passage says soldiers build 'not only muscle but the habit of showing up when motivation fades'."},{"q":"The phrase 'when motivation fades' implies that","options":["discipline replaces all training","goals are unimportant","motivation is temporary but habit endures","soldiers never feel motivated"],"answer":2,"expl":"The contrast shows motivation fades while the trained habit remains."},{"q":"Read the passage and answer: 'The monsoon reached Kerala on schedule, but its advance stalled over central India for nearly two weeks. Meteorologists attribute the pause to a weak cross-equatorial flow and an El Nino-like warming in the Pacific.' The monsoon stalled over","options":["central India","the Pacific","Kerala","the equator"],"answer":0,"expl":"The passage says its advance 'stalled over central India for nearly two weeks'."},{"q":"According to the passage, meteorologists blame the pause on","options":["heavy rain in Kerala","cyclones in the Bay of Bengal","a weak cross-equatorial flow and Pacific warming","deforestation in central India"],"answer":2,"expl":"The passage attributes the pause to 'a weak cross-equatorial flow and an El Nino-like warming in the Pacific'."},{"q":"The synonym of RELUCTANT is","options":["ready","unwilling","eager","willing"],"answer":1,"expl":"'Reluctant' means unwilling or hesitant."},{"q":"The antonym of COMPULSORY is","options":["forced","optional","mandatory","binding"],"answer":1,"expl":"'Compulsory' means required; 'optional' means not required."},{"q":"One word for 'a person who believes in fate' is","options":["fatalist","realist","optimist","pessimist"],"answer":0,"expl":"A fatalist believes events are decided by fate."},{"q":"The idiom 'to cry over spilt milk' means","options":["to weep loudly","to waste time regretting the past","to clean up a mess","to ask for help"],"answer":1,"expl":"It advises against regretting things that cannot be undone."},{"q":"Fill in the blank: She is the ___ girl in the class. (tall)","options":["most tall","taller","tallest","tall"],"answer":2,"expl":"Superlative 'tallest' is used when comparing more than two."},{"q":"Fill in the blank: He is junior ___ me in service.","options":["from","to","than","of"],"answer":1,"expl":"'Junior', 'senior', 'inferior', 'superior' take 'to', not 'than'."},{"q":"Change into indirect speech: He said, 'I saw the match yesterday.'","options":["He said that he had seen the match the previous day.","He said that I had seen the match yesterday.","He said that he saw the match yesterday.","He says that he had seen the match the previous day."],"answer":0,"expl":"Past simple becomes past perfect and 'yesterday' becomes 'the previous day'."},{"q":"Choose the correctly spelt word:","options":["Conscintious","Conscientious","Consientious","Conscientous"],"answer":1,"expl":"'Conscientious' (careful, diligent) is the correct spelling."},{"q":"The synonym of RESILIENT is","options":["weak","rigid","fragile","tough"],"answer":3,"expl":"'Resilient' means able to recover quickly; 'tough' is closest."},{"q":"The antonym of FRAGILE is","options":["brittle","delicate","weak","durable"],"answer":3,"expl":"'Fragile' means easily broken; its opposite is 'durable' (long-lasting)."},{"q":"Identify the error: 'Each boy and each girl have a uniform.' The error is in","options":["'a' should be 'an'","'have' should be 'has'","'uniform' should be 'uniforms'","'each' should be 'every'"],"answer":1,"expl":"'Each' makes the subject singular, so the verb must be 'has'."},{"q":"Fill in the blank: He deals ___ spices.","options":["on","at","with","in"],"answer":3,"expl":"'Deal in' means to trade in goods; 'deal with' means to handle a person or matter."},{"q":"The phrasal verb 'run into' means","options":["manage","collide and flee","meet unexpectedly","enter quickly"],"answer":2,"expl":"'Run into' means to meet someone by chance."},{"q":"One word for 'a book published after the death of its author' is","options":["posthumous","biography","anonymous","manuscript"],"answer":0,"expl":"'Posthumous' describes works appearing after the author's death."},{"q":"The idiom 'to add insult to injury' means","options":["to make a bad situation worse","to praise falsely","to apologise sincerely","to treat a wound"],"answer":0,"expl":"It means worsening an already bad situation with further offence."},{"q":"Choose the correct sentence:","options":["The scissor are sharp.","The scissors has sharp.","The scissors is sharp.","The scissors are sharp."],"answer":3,"expl":"'Scissors' is plural in form and takes a plural verb."},{"q":"Fill in the blank: Neither the teacher nor the students ___ present.","options":["is","has been","was","were"],"answer":3,"expl":"With 'neither...nor', the verb agrees with the nearer subject ('students' - plural)."},{"q":"Fill in the blank: The committee ___ divided on the issue.","options":["were","has","is being","was"],"answer":3,"expl":"'Was divided' treats the committee as a single body split in opinion; with 'divided', singular 'was' is standard."},{"q":"The synonym of DILIGENT is","options":["lazy","careless","idle","hardworking"],"answer":3,"expl":"'Diligent' means showing care and steady effort, i.e. hardworking."},{"q":"The antonym of ARRIVAL is","options":["coming","approach","entry","departure"],"answer":3,"expl":"'Arrival' means reaching a place; its opposite is 'departure'."},{"q":"One word for 'a person who travels on foot' is","options":["nomad","pilgrim","pedestrian","traveller"],"answer":2,"expl":"A pedestrian travels on foot; a nomad wanders and a pilgrim travels for religious reasons."},{"q":"The idiom 'to let the cat out of the bag' means","options":["to free an animal","to waste money","to start a fight","to reveal a secret"],"answer":3,"expl":"It means to disclose a secret, usually by accident."}]},
{"id":"def-bank-physics","title":"Practice Bank — Physics (71 MCQs)","subject":"GAT (Science)","tag":"71 questions · answers with explanations","blurb":"71 exam-style physics MCQs. Pick an option on any question to see the answer and explanation instantly.","intro":"71 exam-style multiple-choice questions on Physics, written for the NDA / CDS / AFCAT / Agniveer written papers. Tap an option to check your answer — the correct answer and a short explanation appear immediately. Everything runs in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere and nothing is saved between visits.","sections":[{"h":"How to use this bank","body":"<p>Work through the questions in timed batches (aim for roughly one question per minute), then read every explanation — including the ones you got right. Patterns repeat across defence papers: the same concept keeps returning in new clothes.</p>"}],"questions":[{"q":"The SI unit of force is the","options":["newton","joule","watt","pascal"],"answer":0,"expl":"Force = mass x acceleration, and its SI unit is the newton (kg m/s^2)."},{"q":"A body moves with uniform velocity. Its acceleration is","options":["constant non-zero","decreasing","zero","increasing"],"answer":2,"expl":"Uniform velocity means no change in velocity, so acceleration is zero."},{"q":"Newton's first law of motion is also called the law of","options":["action-reaction","inertia","gravitation","momentum"],"answer":1,"expl":"The first law states that a body resists changes to its state of motion, i.e. inertia."},{"q":"The momentum of a 2 kg object moving at 3 m/s is","options":["9 kg m/s","1.5 kg m/s","5 kg m/s","6 kg m/s"],"answer":3,"expl":"Momentum p = mv = 2 x 3 = 6 kg m/s."},{"q":"A force of 10 N acting through 5 m does work of","options":["15 J","50 J","2 J","500 J"],"answer":1,"expl":"Work = force x displacement = 10 x 5 = 50 J."},{"q":"The SI unit of power is the","options":["joule","horsepower (SI)","newton","watt"],"answer":3,"expl":"Power is the rate of doing work; its SI unit is the watt (J/s)."},{"q":"1 kilowatt-hour is equal to","options":["3.6 x 10^3 J","1000 J","3600 J","3.6 x 10^6 J"],"answer":3,"expl":"1 kWh = 1000 W x 3600 s = 3.6 x 10^6 J."},{"q":"The kinetic energy of a 2 kg mass moving at 4 m/s is","options":["8 J","4 J","32 J","16 J"],"answer":3,"expl":"KE = 1/2 mv^2 = 0.5 x 2 x 16 = 16 J."},{"q":"The potential energy of a 5 kg mass raised 2 m (g = 10 m/s^2) is","options":["100 J","50 J","10 J","25 J"],"answer":0,"expl":"PE = mgh = 5 x 10 x 2 = 100 J."},{"q":"The value of acceleration due to gravity on Earth is approximately","options":["9.8 km/s^2","6.7 m/s^2","9.8 m/s^2","1.6 m/s^2"],"answer":2,"expl":"g is about 9.8 m/s^2 near Earth's surface."},{"q":"The escape velocity from Earth is approximately","options":["3.0 km/s","9.8 km/s","7.9 km/s","11.2 km/s"],"answer":3,"expl":"Escape velocity = sqrt(2gR), about 11.2 km/s for Earth."},{"q":"A geostationary satellite has a time period of","options":["365 days","90 minutes","12 hours","24 hours"],"answer":3,"expl":"It stays fixed over one point on the equator, matching Earth's 24-hour rotation."},{"q":"The time period of a simple pendulum depends on","options":["the mass of the bob","its length","the amplitude (for small angles, no)","the material of the string"],"answer":1,"expl":"T = 2 pi sqrt(l/g); for small oscillations it depends only on length and g."},{"q":"Sound waves are","options":["electromagnetic","transverse","longitudinal","stationary only"],"answer":2,"expl":"Sound propagates as compressions and rarefactions along the direction of travel."},{"q":"The speed of sound in air at room temperature is approximately","options":["343 m/s","3 x 10^8 m/s","1500 m/s","330 km/s"],"answer":0,"expl":"Sound travels about 343 m/s in air at 20 degrees C."},{"q":"Ultrasonic waves have frequencies","options":["equal to 20 Hz","above 20 kHz","below 20 Hz","between 20 Hz and 20 kHz"],"answer":1,"expl":"Ultrasound is above the human hearing range of 20 Hz - 20 kHz."},{"q":"The SI unit of frequency is the","options":["tesla","watt","decibel","hertz"],"answer":3,"expl":"Frequency is measured in hertz (cycles per second)."},{"q":"Light travels fastest in","options":["vacuum","glass","diamond","water"],"answer":0,"expl":"Light's maximum speed c = 3 x 10^8 m/s occurs in vacuum."},{"q":"The speed of light in vacuum is","options":["3 x 10^10 m/s","3 x 10^6 m/s","3 x 10^8 m/s","1.5 x 10^8 m/s"],"answer":2,"expl":"c is exactly 299,792,458 m/s, about 3 x 10^8 m/s."},{"q":"A convex lens is also called a","options":["cylindrical lens","diverging lens","converging lens","plano lens"],"answer":2,"expl":"A convex lens brings parallel rays together to a focus."},{"q":"The image formed by a plane mirror is","options":["virtual, erect and same-sized","virtual and diminished","real and erect","real and inverted"],"answer":0,"expl":"A plane mirror forms a virtual, erect image of the same size as the object."},{"q":"Total internal reflection occurs when light travels from","options":["denser to rarer medium beyond the critical angle","air to water at any angle","rarer to denser medium","vacuum to glass"],"answer":0,"expl":"TIR needs travel from denser to rarer medium with incidence above the critical angle."},{"q":"The phenomenon responsible for the blue colour of the sky is","options":["dispersion","diffraction","total internal reflection","scattering of light"],"answer":3,"expl":"Rayleigh scattering scatters shorter (blue) wavelengths most strongly."},{"q":"A rainbow is formed due to","options":["polarisation only","diffraction only","interference only","dispersion, refraction and internal reflection"],"answer":3,"expl":"Sunlight disperses in raindrops via refraction, internal reflection and refraction again."},{"q":"The SI unit of electric charge is the","options":["ampere","coulomb","volt","ohm"],"answer":1,"expl":"Charge is measured in coulombs; the ampere is charge per second."},{"q":"Ohm's law states that V equals","options":["IR","I/R","R/I","I^2 R"],"answer":0,"expl":"Voltage = current x resistance is the statement of Ohm's law."},{"q":"The SI unit of electrical resistance is the","options":["siemens","volt","ohm","ampere"],"answer":2,"expl":"Resistance is measured in ohms (symbol omega)."},{"q":"In a series circuit, the quantity that remains the same through each resistor is","options":["charge density","voltage","current","power"],"answer":2,"expl":"Series components carry the same current; voltage divides across them."},{"q":"The power dissipated in a resistor is given by","options":["V^2/R","I/R","V x R","V/R^2"],"answer":0,"expl":"P = VI = V^2/R = I^2 R for a resistor."},{"q":"A transformer works on the principle of","options":["mutual induction","self induction only","Coulomb's law","Ohm's law"],"answer":0,"expl":"A changing current in the primary induces voltage in the secondary by mutual induction."},{"q":"The SI unit of magnetic field (flux density) is the","options":["tesla","weber","henry","gauss (SI)"],"answer":0,"expl":"Magnetic flux density B is measured in tesla."},{"q":"Fleming's left-hand rule is used to find the direction of","options":["force on a current-carrying conductor","induced current","electric field","magnetic field lines"],"answer":0,"expl":"The left-hand rule gives force (motor rule); the right-hand rule gives induced current."},{"q":"The device that converts AC to DC is a","options":["rectifier","amplifier","transformer","inverter"],"answer":0,"expl":"A rectifier allows current in one direction only, converting AC to DC."},{"q":"The half-life of a radioactive substance is the time for","options":["one quarter to decay","the activity to double","half the nuclei to decay","all nuclei to decay"],"answer":2,"expl":"Half-life is defined as the time for half the undecayed nuclei to disintegrate."},{"q":"In a nuclear reactor, the moderator is used to","options":["speed up neutrons","cool the fuel electrically","slow down neutrons","absorb all radiation"],"answer":2,"expl":"Slow (thermal) neutrons sustain fission in U-235; graphite or heavy water moderates them."},{"q":"The energy source of the Sun is","options":["chemical combustion","gravitational contraction","nuclear fusion","nuclear fission"],"answer":2,"expl":"The Sun fuses hydrogen into helium in its core, releasing enormous energy."},{"q":"The photoelectric effect was explained by","options":["Einstein","Newton","Maxwell","Huygens"],"answer":0,"expl":"Einstein explained it with light quanta (photons), winning the 1921 Nobel Prize."},{"q":"The SI unit of temperature is the","options":["fahrenheit","celsius","rankine","kelvin"],"answer":3,"expl":"Kelvin is the SI base unit of thermodynamic temperature."},{"q":"Heat transfer by the actual movement of fluid particles is called","options":["radiation","convection","advection only","conduction"],"answer":1,"expl":"Convection carries heat via bulk motion of fluids; conduction needs direct contact."},{"q":"A black body is a","options":["perfect reflector","body painted black","perfect absorber and emitter of radiation","perfect transmitter"],"answer":2,"expl":"An ideal black body absorbs all incident radiation and emits maximally."},{"q":"The specific heat capacity of water is about","options":["1000 J/(kg K)","418 J/(kg K)","4200 J/(kg K)","900 J/(kg K)"],"answer":2,"expl":"Water's specific heat is about 4.2 kJ/(kg K), unusually high."},{"q":"When ice melts at 0 C, the heat absorbed is called","options":["sensible heat","latent heat of fusion","latent heat of vaporisation","specific heat"],"answer":1,"expl":"Latent heat of fusion melts ice without changing temperature."},{"q":"The pressure at a depth h in a liquid of density rho is","options":["rho h / g","g h / rho","rho g h","rho g / h"],"answer":2,"expl":"Hydrostatic pressure p = rho g h."},{"q":"An object floats when its average density is","options":["less than the fluid's density","independent of volume","greater than the fluid's density","equal to vacuum"],"answer":0,"expl":"Buoyancy exceeds weight when the body is less dense than the fluid (Archimedes)."},{"q":"The SI unit of pressure is the","options":["atmosphere","pascal","bar","torr (SI)"],"answer":1,"expl":"Pressure = force/area; its SI unit is the pascal (N/m^2)."},{"q":"Bernoulli's principle relates","options":["charge and voltage","force and acceleration","temperature and volume","pressure and velocity of a flowing fluid"],"answer":3,"expl":"Faster flow means lower pressure along a streamline (for ideal fluids)."},{"q":"The dimensional formula of force is","options":["[M L T^-1]","[M L^2 T^-2]","[M L^-1 T^-2]","[M L T^-2]"],"answer":3,"expl":"Force = mass x acceleration gives M x L T^-2."},{"q":"A vector quantity among the following is","options":["speed","distance","velocity","energy"],"answer":2,"expl":"Velocity has magnitude and direction; speed, distance and energy are scalars."},{"q":"The work done by a centripetal force on a moving body is","options":["negative","equal to kinetic energy","zero","maximum"],"answer":2,"expl":"Centripetal force is perpendicular to displacement, so no work is done."},{"q":"A body in uniform circular motion has","options":["zero acceleration","tangential acceleration only","constant velocity","centripetal acceleration towards the centre"],"answer":3,"expl":"Direction changes continuously, giving acceleration v^2/r towards the centre."},{"q":"The SI unit of angular velocity is","options":["rev/s (SI)","rad/s","degree/s (SI)","hertz only"],"answer":1,"expl":"Angular velocity is measured in radians per second."},{"q":"Hooke's law states that stress is proportional to","options":["strain","volume","area","temperature"],"answer":0,"expl":"Within the elastic limit, stress/strain = modulus of elasticity."},{"q":"The SI unit of surface tension is","options":["N/m","N m","J/m^2 only","Pa s"],"answer":0,"expl":"Surface tension is force per unit length: N/m."},{"q":"Viscosity is a measure of a fluid's","options":["density","resistance to flow","surface area","compressibility"],"answer":1,"expl":"Viscosity quantifies internal friction opposing relative motion of layers."},{"q":"A concave mirror can form","options":["only virtual images","only real images","no image at all","both real and virtual images"],"answer":3,"expl":"Depending on object position, a concave mirror forms real or virtual images."},{"q":"The focal length of a plane mirror is","options":["zero","infinity","half its width","equal to its radius"],"answer":1,"expl":"A plane mirror has zero curvature, hence infinite focal length."},{"q":"Myopia is corrected using a","options":["prism","cylindrical mirror","concave lens","convex lens"],"answer":2,"expl":"Short-sightedness needs a diverging (concave) lens to focus distant images on the retina."},{"q":"Hypermetropia is corrected using a","options":["concave lens","bifocal mirror","plane glass","convex lens"],"answer":3,"expl":"Long-sightedness needs a converging (convex) lens."},{"q":"The primary colours of light are","options":["red, green and blue","cyan, magenta and yellow","red, yellow and blue","violet, indigo and red"],"answer":0,"expl":"Additive primaries of light are RGB; mixing them gives white."},{"q":"An electric motor converts","options":["mechanical to electrical","light to electrical","electrical energy to mechanical energy","heat to electrical"],"answer":2,"expl":"A motor uses the force on current-carrying conductors in a magnetic field to do work."},{"q":"An electric generator converts","options":["mechanical energy to electrical energy","electrical to mechanical","solar to electrical","chemical to electrical"],"answer":0,"expl":"A generator uses electromagnetic induction from mechanical rotation."},{"q":"The frequency of AC mains supply in India is","options":["60 Hz","100 Hz","50 Hz","220 Hz"],"answer":2,"expl":"India's domestic supply is 230 V at 50 Hz."},{"q":"The filament of an electric bulb is made of","options":["copper","tungsten","nichrome (bulb)","aluminium"],"answer":1,"expl":"Tungsten's very high melting point suits incandescent filaments."},{"q":"Fuse wire is made of an alloy with","options":["high melting point","high resistance only","low melting point","magnetic properties"],"answer":2,"expl":"The fuse must melt quickly when current exceeds the safe limit."},{"q":"The SI unit of electric capacitance is the","options":["volt","farad","henry","coulomb"],"answer":1,"expl":"Capacitance C = Q/V is measured in farads."},{"q":"Electromagnetic waves were predicted by","options":["Hertz","Faraday","Marconi","Maxwell"],"answer":3,"expl":"Maxwell's equations predicted EM waves; Hertz demonstrated them experimentally."},{"q":"The electromagnetic wave with the longest wavelength among the following is","options":["radio waves","microwaves","visible light","infrared"],"answer":0,"expl":"Radio waves have the longest wavelengths in the EM spectrum."},{"q":"X-rays were discovered by","options":["Becquerel","Curie","Roentgen","Rutherford"],"answer":2,"expl":"Wilhelm Roentgen discovered X-rays in 1895."},{"q":"The penetrating power is greatest for","options":["gamma rays","alpha particles","protons (same energy)","beta particles"],"answer":0,"expl":"Gamma rays, being chargeless EM radiation, penetrate deepest."},{"q":"In the Bohr model, the angular momentum of an electron is","options":["an integral multiple of h/2 pi","continuous","equal to h","zero"],"answer":0,"expl":"Bohr's quantisation: mvr = n h/(2 pi)."},{"q":"The SI unit of luminous intensity is the","options":["lumen","lux","candela","watt"],"answer":2,"expl":"Candela is the SI base unit for luminous intensity."}]},
{"id":"def-bank-chemistry","title":"Practice Bank — Chemistry (40 MCQs)","subject":"GAT (Science)","tag":"40 questions · answers with explanations","blurb":"40 exam-style chemistry MCQs. Pick an option on any question to see the answer and explanation instantly.","intro":"40 exam-style multiple-choice questions on Chemistry, written for the NDA / CDS / AFCAT / Agniveer written papers. Tap an option to check your answer — the correct answer and a short explanation appear immediately. Everything runs in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere and nothing is saved between visits.","sections":[{"h":"How to use this bank","body":"<p>Work through the questions in timed batches (aim for roughly one question per minute), then read every explanation — including the ones you got right. Patterns repeat across defence papers: the same concept keeps returning in new clothes.</p>"}],"questions":[{"q":"The atomic number of an element equals the number of","options":["protons","neutrons","electrons + neutrons","nucleons only"],"answer":0,"expl":"Atomic number Z is the count of protons in the nucleus."},{"q":"Isotopes of an element have the same number of","options":["neutrons","electrons + neutrons","protons","nucleons"],"answer":2,"expl":"Isotopes differ in neutrons but share the same proton count."},{"q":"The most abundant element in the Earth's crust is","options":["iron","oxygen","aluminium","silicon"],"answer":1,"expl":"Oxygen makes up about 46% of the crust by mass."},{"q":"The most abundant gas in the Earth's atmosphere is","options":["oxygen","nitrogen","argon","carbon dioxide"],"answer":1,"expl":"Nitrogen is about 78% of dry air by volume."},{"q":"The pH of a neutral solution at 25 C is","options":["7","14","1","0"],"answer":0,"expl":"Neutral pH is 7; below 7 is acidic and above 7 is basic."},{"q":"The pH of lemon juice is approximately","options":["13","7","2","10"],"answer":2,"expl":"Lemon juice is acidic with pH around 2-3."},{"q":"Common salt is chemically","options":["sodium bicarbonate","sodium carbonate","sodium chloride","potassium chloride"],"answer":2,"expl":"Table salt is NaCl."},{"q":"Baking soda is chemically","options":["sodium bicarbonate","calcium carbonate","sodium carbonate","sodium hydroxide"],"answer":0,"expl":"Baking soda is NaHCO3."},{"q":"Washing soda is chemically","options":["sodium bicarbonate","sodium carbonate","sodium chloride","calcium carbonate"],"answer":1,"expl":"Washing soda is Na2CO3 (usually the decahydrate)."},{"q":"The chemical formula of quicklime is","options":["CaO","Ca(OH)2","CaCl2","CaCO3"],"answer":0,"expl":"Quicklime is calcium oxide, CaO."},{"q":"The gas released when an acid reacts with a metal is","options":["hydrogen","carbon dioxide","oxygen","chlorine"],"answer":0,"expl":"Acids liberate H2 with active metals (e.g. Zn + HCl)."},{"q":"The pH of human blood is approximately","options":["6.8","8.5","7.0","7.4"],"answer":3,"expl":"Blood is slightly alkaline, maintained around pH 7.35-7.45."},{"q":"The metal that is liquid at room temperature is","options":["mercury","iron","sodium","aluminium"],"answer":0,"expl":"Mercury is the only metal liquid at room temperature."},{"q":"The non-metal that is liquid at room temperature is","options":["chlorine","sulphur","iodine","bromine"],"answer":3,"expl":"Bromine is a fuming reddish-brown liquid at room temperature."},{"q":"The hardest natural substance is","options":["quartz","diamond","graphite","corundum"],"answer":1,"expl":"Diamond (carbon) tops the Mohs hardness scale at 10."},{"q":"Graphite is a good conductor of electricity because","options":["it has free delocalised electrons","it is magnetic","it has free protons","it is a metal"],"answer":0,"expl":"Each carbon in graphite contributes a delocalised pi electron."},{"q":"The gas used in balloons and as a coolant (inert, light) is","options":["helium","hydrogen","nitrogen","neon"],"answer":0,"expl":"Helium is light and non-flammable, ideal for balloons."},{"q":"The main gas responsible for the greenhouse effect enhancement is","options":["nitrogen","oxygen","helium","carbon dioxide"],"answer":3,"expl":"CO2 is the principal anthropogenic greenhouse gas."},{"q":"The chemical formula of water is H2O; heavy water is","options":["D2O","T2O only","HDO only","H2O2"],"answer":0,"expl":"Heavy water uses deuterium (D) instead of ordinary hydrogen."},{"q":"The number of moles in 18 g of water is","options":["2","0.5","1","18"],"answer":2,"expl":"Molar mass of H2O is 18 g/mol, so 18 g is 1 mole."},{"q":"Avogadro's number is approximately","options":["9.1 x 10^-31","6.022 x 10^23","1.6 x 10^-19","3.011 x 10^23"],"answer":1,"expl":"One mole contains 6.022 x 10^23 entities."},{"q":"An element with atomic number 6 is","options":["nitrogen","carbon","boron","oxygen"],"answer":1,"expl":"Z = 6 is carbon."},{"q":"The electronic configuration of sodium (Z = 11) is","options":["2, 8, 2","2, 7, 2","2, 8, 1","2, 8, 8"],"answer":2,"expl":"Sodium has 11 electrons: K=2, L=8, M=1."},{"q":"Elements in the same group of the periodic table have the same number of","options":["neutrons","isotopes","electron shells","valence electrons"],"answer":3,"expl":"Group number reflects valence-electron count, giving similar chemistry."},{"q":"The most electronegative element is","options":["fluorine","oxygen","caesium","chlorine"],"answer":0,"expl":"Fluorine has the highest electronegativity (3.98 on the Pauling scale)."},{"q":"Noble gases are chemically inert because they have","options":["a complete outer shell","metallic character","no electrons","only one shell"],"answer":0,"expl":"A full valence shell (octet) makes them stable and unreactive."},{"q":"A covalent bond is formed by","options":["sharing of protons","transfer of electrons","sharing of electrons","transfer of neutrons"],"answer":2,"expl":"Covalent bonds share electron pairs between atoms."},{"q":"An ionic bond is formed by","options":["sharing of neutrons","transfer of electrons","metallic sea only","sharing of electrons"],"answer":1,"expl":"Ionic bonds form when electrons transfer from metal to non-metal."},{"q":"Vinegar contains","options":["formic acid","sulphuric acid","acetic acid","citric acid"],"answer":2,"expl":"Vinegar is a dilute solution of acetic acid (~5%)."},{"q":"The acid in car batteries is","options":["sulphuric acid","nitric acid","hydrochloric acid","phosphoric acid"],"answer":0,"expl":"Lead-acid batteries use dilute sulphuric acid as electrolyte."},{"q":"The gas that turns limewater milky is","options":["nitrogen","hydrogen","oxygen","carbon dioxide"],"answer":3,"expl":"CO2 forms insoluble calcium carbonate with limewater."},{"q":"Rusting of iron requires","options":["carbon dioxide only","oxygen only","oxygen and moisture","sunlight only"],"answer":2,"expl":"Rusting is oxidation needing both air (oxygen) and water."},{"q":"Galvanisation protects iron by coating it with","options":["zinc","copper","tin","aluminium"],"answer":0,"expl":"A zinc coating corrodes preferentially, protecting the iron."},{"q":"Brass is an alloy of","options":["copper and zinc","iron and carbon","copper and tin","aluminium and copper"],"answer":0,"expl":"Brass = Cu + Zn; bronze = Cu + Sn."},{"q":"Bronze is an alloy of","options":["lead and tin","copper and tin","iron and nickel","copper and zinc"],"answer":1,"expl":"Bronze is copper alloyed mainly with tin."},{"q":"Stainless steel resists corrosion because it contains","options":["lead","zinc","chromium","tin"],"answer":2,"expl":"Chromium forms a protective oxide layer on the steel."},{"q":"The main component of natural gas is","options":["butane","propane","ethane","methane"],"answer":3,"expl":"Natural gas is mostly methane (CH4)."},{"q":"LPG (cooking gas) mainly contains","options":["hydrogen and CO","propane and butane","methane and ethane","acetylene"],"answer":1,"expl":"LPG is liquefied propane + butane."},{"q":"The functional group -OH belongs to","options":["acids","aldehydes","alcohols","ketones"],"answer":2,"expl":"The hydroxyl group -OH characterises alcohols."},{"q":"Ethanol's chemical formula is","options":["C2H4","CH3OH","C2H5OH","C3H7OH"],"answer":2,"expl":"Ethanol is C2H5OH (drinking alcohol)."}]},
{"id":"def-bank-biology","title":"Practice Bank — Biology (45 MCQs)","subject":"GAT (Science)","tag":"45 questions · answers with explanations","blurb":"45 exam-style biology MCQs. Pick an option on any question to see the answer and explanation instantly.","intro":"45 exam-style multiple-choice questions on Biology, written for the NDA / CDS / AFCAT / Agniveer written papers. Tap an option to check your answer — the correct answer and a short explanation appear immediately. Everything runs in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere and nothing is saved between visits.","sections":[{"h":"How to use this bank","body":"<p>Work through the questions in timed batches (aim for roughly one question per minute), then read every explanation — including the ones you got right. Patterns repeat across defence papers: the same concept keeps returning in new clothes.</p>"}],"questions":[{"q":"The powerhouse of the cell is the","options":["chloroplast","nucleus","ribosome","mitochondrion"],"answer":3,"expl":"Mitochondria produce ATP, the cell's energy currency."},{"q":"Photosynthesis occurs in the","options":["mitochondrion","vacuole","nucleus","chloroplast"],"answer":3,"expl":"Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll, which captures light energy."},{"q":"The control centre of the cell is the","options":["cytoplasm","nucleus","cell wall","lysosome"],"answer":1,"expl":"The nucleus holds DNA and directs all cell activities."},{"q":"Protein synthesis takes place in the","options":["nucleus","Golgi body","lysosome","ribosome"],"answer":3,"expl":"Ribosomes assemble amino acids into proteins."},{"q":"The basic structural and functional unit of life is the","options":["cell","tissue","atom","organ"],"answer":0,"expl":"All living organisms are made of cells."},{"q":"Red blood cells are produced in the","options":["heart","spleen","liver","bone marrow"],"answer":3,"expl":"Red bone marrow produces RBCs, WBCs and platelets."},{"q":"The normal human body temperature is about","options":["37 C","35 C","40 C","39 C"],"answer":0,"expl":"37 C (98.6 F) is the standard core body temperature."},{"q":"The largest bone in the human body is the","options":["tibia","skull","humerus","femur"],"answer":3,"expl":"The femur (thigh bone) is the longest and strongest bone."},{"q":"The smallest bone in the human body is the","options":["stapes","phalanx","incus","malleus"],"answer":0,"expl":"The stapes in the middle ear is about 3 mm long."},{"q":"The number of bones in an adult human body is","options":["256","206","106","306"],"answer":1,"expl":"Adults have 206 bones; babies are born with about 300."},{"q":"The functional unit of the kidney is the","options":["neuron","alveolus","nephron","glomerulus only"],"answer":2,"expl":"Each kidney has about a million nephrons filtering blood."},{"q":"The functional unit of the nervous system is the","options":["axon only","nephron","neuron","synapse only"],"answer":2,"expl":"Neurons carry nerve impulses; the nephron belongs to the kidney."},{"q":"Exchange of gases in the lungs occurs in the","options":["trachea","bronchi","diaphragm","alveoli"],"answer":3,"expl":"Tiny air sacs (alveoli) are where O2 and CO2 exchange with blood."},{"q":"The normal pH range of human blood is","options":["7.0 exactly","7.8 - 8.2","6.8 - 7.0","7.35 - 7.45"],"answer":3,"expl":"Blood is slightly alkaline, tightly regulated around 7.4."},{"q":"Haemoglobin is rich in","options":["zinc","iron","calcium","magnesium"],"answer":1,"expl":"Each haemoglobin molecule carries iron that binds oxygen."},{"q":"The universal blood donor group is","options":["O negative","O positive","A negative","AB positive"],"answer":0,"expl":"O negative RBCs lack A, B and Rh antigens, so they suit all recipients."},{"q":"The universal blood recipient group is","options":["A positive","B positive","AB positive","O negative"],"answer":2,"expl":"AB positive has all antigens and no antibodies against donor blood."},{"q":"Insulin is secreted by the","options":["adrenal gland","liver","thyroid","pancreas"],"answer":3,"expl":"Beta cells of the pancreatic islets secrete insulin."},{"q":"Diabetes is caused by deficiency or ineffectiveness of","options":["adrenaline","insulin","growth hormone","thyroxine"],"answer":1,"expl":"Without effective insulin, blood sugar stays high."},{"q":"The thyroid gland needs","options":["iodine","calcium","zinc","iron"],"answer":0,"expl":"Iodine is essential for making thyroxine; its lack causes goitre."},{"q":"Vitamin D deficiency causes","options":["night blindness","rickets","scurvy","beriberi"],"answer":1,"expl":"Rickets softens bones in children lacking vitamin D/calcium."},{"q":"Vitamin C deficiency causes","options":["anaemia only","scurvy","rickets","goitre"],"answer":1,"expl":"Scurvy (bleeding gums, weak vessels) comes from lack of vitamin C."},{"q":"Night blindness is caused by deficiency of","options":["vitamin K","vitamin C","vitamin B","vitamin A"],"answer":3,"expl":"Vitamin A maintains the retina's light-sensitive pigments."},{"q":"Beriberi is caused by deficiency of","options":["vitamin B1","vitamin B12","vitamin E","vitamin D"],"answer":0,"expl":"Lack of thiamine (B1) causes beriberi."},{"q":"The vitamin that helps blood clotting is","options":["vitamin K","vitamin D","vitamin A","vitamin C"],"answer":0,"expl":"Vitamin K is needed to make clotting factors."},{"q":"The part of the brain controlling balance is the","options":["cerebellum","medulla","hypothalamus","cerebrum"],"answer":0,"expl":"The cerebellum coordinates movement and balance."},{"q":"The largest part of the human brain is the","options":["pons","cerebellum","thalamus","cerebrum"],"answer":3,"expl":"The cerebrum handles thought, memory and voluntary action."},{"q":"DNA stands for","options":["deoxyribonucleic acid","diribonucleic acid","dynamic nucleic acid","deoxyribose nucleic amine"],"answer":0,"expl":"DNA = deoxyribonucleic acid, the hereditary molecule."},{"q":"The double-helix model of DNA was proposed by","options":["Morgan and Beadle","Darwin and Wallace","Watson and Crick","Lamarck and Mendel"],"answer":2,"expl":"Watson and Crick published the double helix in 1953."},{"q":"Mendel is known as the father of","options":["evolution","ecology","genetics","taxonomy"],"answer":2,"expl":"Mendel's pea-plant experiments founded genetics."},{"q":"The theory of natural selection was proposed by","options":["Louis Pasteur","Charles Darwin","Gregor Mendel","Robert Hooke"],"answer":1,"expl":"Darwin's 'On the Origin of Species' (1859) proposed natural selection."},{"q":"Malaria is caused by","options":["a virus","a fungus","Plasmodium","a bacterium"],"answer":2,"expl":"The Plasmodium parasite, spread by female Anopheles mosquitoes, causes malaria."},{"q":"The vector of dengue is the","options":["Anopheles mosquito","Aedes mosquito","housefly","Culex mosquito"],"answer":1,"expl":"Aedes aegypti spreads dengue, chikungunya and Zika."},{"q":"Tuberculosis is caused by","options":["a virus","a fungus","a protozoan","a bacterium (Mycobacterium tuberculosis)"],"answer":3,"expl":"TB is a bacterial infection, curable with antibiotics."},{"q":"The BCG vaccine protects against","options":["typhoid","tuberculosis","polio","measles"],"answer":1,"expl":"BCG (Bacille Calmette-Guerin) is given at birth against TB."},{"q":"Cholera is caused by","options":["Salmonella typhi","Vibrio cholerae","Shigella","E. coli"],"answer":1,"expl":"Cholera is a bacterial water-borne disease causing severe diarrhoea."},{"q":"Typhoid is caused by","options":["Mycobacterium","Plasmodium","Vibrio cholerae","Salmonella typhi"],"answer":3,"expl":"Typhoid spreads through contaminated food and water."},{"q":"The process by which plants lose water as vapour is","options":["respiration","photosynthesis","transpiration","guttation only"],"answer":2,"expl":"Transpiration is water loss mainly through leaf stomata."},{"q":"The green pigment in leaves is","options":["chlorophyll","carotene only","melanin","haemoglobin"],"answer":0,"expl":"Chlorophyll absorbs light for photosynthesis."},{"q":"Stomata are mainly found on the","options":["roots","upper surface of leaves","lower surface of leaves","stem bark"],"answer":2,"expl":"Most stomata sit on the underside of leaves to reduce water loss."},{"q":"The male reproductive part of a flower is the","options":["stamen","petal","pistil","sepal"],"answer":0,"expl":"The stamen (anther + filament) produces pollen."},{"q":"The female reproductive part of a flower is the","options":["corolla","calyx","stamen","pistil"],"answer":3,"expl":"The pistil (stigma, style, ovary) receives pollen."},{"q":"The study of fungi is called","options":["entomology","ornithology","mycology","phycology"],"answer":2,"expl":"Mycology studies fungi; phycology studies algae."},{"q":"The study of insects is called","options":["herpetology","ornithology","entomology","ichthyology"],"answer":2,"expl":"Entomology is the science of insects."},{"q":"A food chain always starts with","options":["decomposers","carnivores","consumers","producers"],"answer":3,"expl":"Producers (green plants) convert solar energy first."}]},
{"id":"def-bank-history","title":"Practice Bank — History (55 MCQs)","subject":"GAT (GK)","tag":"55 questions · answers with explanations","blurb":"55 exam-style history MCQs. Pick an option on any question to see the answer and explanation instantly.","intro":"55 exam-style multiple-choice questions on History, written for the NDA / CDS / AFCAT / Agniveer written papers. Tap an option to check your answer — the correct answer and a short explanation appear immediately. Everything runs in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere and nothing is saved between visits.","sections":[{"h":"How to use this bank","body":"<p>Work through the questions in timed batches (aim for roughly one question per minute), then read every explanation — including the ones you got right. Patterns repeat across defence papers: the same concept keeps returning in new clothes.</p>"}],"questions":[{"q":"The Indus Valley Civilisation is also known as the","options":["Harappan Civilisation","Aryan Civilisation","Vedic Civilisation","Mauryan Civilisation"],"answer":0,"expl":"Named after Harappa, the first site excavated, it is the mature Harappan phase."},{"q":"Mohenjo-daro is located in present-day","options":["Pakistan (Sindh)","Gujarat","Rajasthan","Punjab (India)"],"answer":0,"expl":"Mohenjo-daro lies in Larkana district, Sindh, Pakistan."},{"q":"The Great Bath was discovered at","options":["Harappa","Kalibangan","Lothal","Mohenjo-daro"],"answer":3,"expl":"The watertight Great Bath is Mohenjo-daro's most famous structure."},{"q":"Lothal, the dockyard site of the Harappans, is in","options":["Haryana","Rajasthan","Gujarat","Punjab"],"answer":2,"expl":"Lothal in Gujarat had a tidal dockyard for sea trade."},{"q":"The Vedas are written in","options":["Tamil","Sanskrit","Prakrit","Pali"],"answer":1,"expl":"The four Vedas were composed in Vedic Sanskrit."},{"q":"The oldest Veda is the","options":["Rig Veda","Sama Veda","Yajur Veda","Atharva Veda"],"answer":0,"expl":"The Rig Veda, a collection of hymns, is the earliest."},{"q":"Gautama Buddha was born at","options":["Bodh Gaya","Sarnath","Lumbini","Kushinagar"],"answer":2,"expl":"Lumbini (in present-day Nepal) is the Buddha's birthplace."},{"q":"Buddha attained enlightenment at","options":["Lumbini","Sarnath","Rajgir","Bodh Gaya"],"answer":3,"expl":"He attained nirvana under the Bodhi tree at Bodh Gaya."},{"q":"Buddha delivered his first sermon at","options":["Bodh Gaya","Kushinagar","Sarnath","Vaishali"],"answer":2,"expl":"The Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta was preached at Sarnath."},{"q":"Mahavira, the 24th Tirthankara of Jainism, was born at","options":["Shravanabelagola","Kundagrama (Vaishali)","Rajgir","Pava"],"answer":1,"expl":"Mahavira was born at Kundagrama near Vaishali."},{"q":"Alexander the Great invaded India in","options":["326 BC","321 BC","323 BC","305 BC"],"answer":0,"expl":"Alexander crossed into India in 326 BC and fought Porus at Hydaspes."},{"q":"Chandragupta Maurya founded the Mauryan Empire in","options":["326 BC","321 BC","273 BC","185 BC"],"answer":1,"expl":"With Chanakya's guidance he overthrew the Nandas around 321 BC."},{"q":"The Arthashastra was written by","options":["Kalidasa","Kautilya (Chanakya)","Banabhatta","Patanjali"],"answer":1,"expl":"Kautilya, Chandragupta's minister, authored the Arthashastra."},{"q":"Ashoka's conversion to Buddhism followed the","options":["Kalinga War","Battle of Hydaspes","Second Buddhist Council","Seleucid War"],"answer":0,"expl":"The slaughter at Kalinga (261 BC) turned Ashoka towards dhamma."},{"q":"Ashoka's edicts were deciphered by","options":["Max Mueller","Alexander Cunningham","James Prinsep","William Jones"],"answer":2,"expl":"Prinsep deciphered the Brahmi script of Ashokan edicts in 1837."},{"q":"The Gupta ruler known as the 'Napoleon of India' was","options":["Samudragupta","Skandagupta","Kumaragupta","Chandragupta II"],"answer":0,"expl":"Historian V. A. Smith gave Samudragupta this title for his conquests."},{"q":"The Iron Pillar at Mehrauli belongs to the reign of","options":["Samudragupta","Chandragupta II","Harsha","Ashoka"],"answer":1,"expl":"The rust-free pillar's inscription names King Chandra, identified as Chandragupta II."},{"q":"Harshavardhana's capital was at","options":["Kannauj","Pataliputra","Thanesar only","Ujjain"],"answer":0,"expl":"Harsha ruled from Kannauj after shifting from Thanesar."},{"q":"The Chinese traveller who visited India during Harsha's reign was","options":["Marco Polo","I-Tsing","Fa-Hien","Hiuen Tsang"],"answer":3,"expl":"Hiuen Tsang (Xuanzang) described Harsha's India in 'Si-yu-ki'."},{"q":"The First Battle of Tarain (1191) was fought between","options":["Rana Sanga and Babur","Hemu and Akbar","Prithviraj Chauhan and Muhammad Ghori","Prithviraj and Mahmud Ghazni"],"answer":2,"expl":"Prithviraj defeated Ghori in 1191; Ghori won the second battle in 1192."},{"q":"The Delhi Sultanate was founded by","options":["Iltutmish","Balban","Alauddin Khalji","Qutb-ud-din Aibak"],"answer":3,"expl":"Aibak founded the Slave (Mamluk) dynasty in 1206."},{"q":"The Qutub Minar was completed by","options":["Alauddin Khalji","Qutb-ud-din Aibak","Firoz Shah Tughlaq","Iltutmish"],"answer":3,"expl":"Aibak began it; Iltutmish completed the tower."},{"q":"Alauddin Khalji is best known for his","options":["Mongol alliance","naval expeditions","market control and price regulation","religious tolerance"],"answer":2,"expl":"His market reforms fixed prices of essentials to support his army."},{"q":"Muhammad bin Tughlaq is remembered for","options":["founding the Mughal Empire","building Fatehpur Sikri","abolishing jizya","shifting the capital to Daulatabad"],"answer":3,"expl":"His transfer of the capital from Delhi to Daulatabad failed famously."},{"q":"The Vijayanagara Empire was founded in 1336 by","options":["Devaraya I","Rajaraja Chola","Harihara and Bukka","Krishnadevaraya"],"answer":2,"expl":"The Sangama brothers founded Vijayanagara on the Tungabhadra."},{"q":"Krishnadevaraya belonged to the","options":["Aravidu dynasty","Tuluva dynasty","Saluva dynasty","Sangama dynasty"],"answer":1,"expl":"The greatest Vijayanagara king (r. 1509-1529) was of the Tuluva line."},{"q":"Babur founded the Mughal Empire after the","options":["Battle of Khanwa","Battle of Chanderi","First Battle of Panipat (1526)","Battle of Ghaghra"],"answer":2,"expl":"Babur defeated Ibrahim Lodi at Panipat in 1526."},{"q":"Babur's autobiography is called","options":["Akbarnama","Padshahnama","Baburnama (Tuzuk-i-Baburi)","Humayunnama"],"answer":2,"expl":"Written in Chagatai Turkish, it is the Baburnama."},{"q":"The Second Battle of Panipat (1556) was between","options":["Babur and Rana Sanga","Sher Shah and Humayun","Ahmad Shah Abdali and Marathas","Akbar and Hemu"],"answer":3,"expl":"Akbar's forces defeated Hemu at Panipat in 1556."},{"q":"Akbar's revenue minister who devised the zabt system was","options":["Abul Fazl","Birbal","Todar Mal","Man Singh"],"answer":2,"expl":"Raja Todar Mal's dahsala/zabt system standardised land revenue."},{"q":"The Buland Darwaza was built by Akbar to commemorate","options":["victory over Hemu","the birth of Salim","the founding of Din-i-Ilahi","the conquest of Gujarat"],"answer":3,"expl":"The great gate at Fatehpur Sikri marks the 1573 Gujarat victory."},{"q":"Shah Jahan built the Taj Mahal in memory of","options":["Jahanara","Nur Jahan","Roshanara","Mumtaz Mahal"],"answer":3,"expl":"The Taj at Agra is Mumtaz Mahal's mausoleum."},{"q":"The Mughal emperor who reimposed jizya was","options":["Shah Jahan","Bahadur Shah I","Jahangir","Aurangzeb"],"answer":3,"expl":"Aurangzeb reimposed the jizya tax on non-Muslims in 1679."},{"q":"Shivaji was crowned Chhatrapati in","options":["1674","1680","1664","1659"],"answer":0,"expl":"The coronation at Raigad took place in 1674."},{"q":"Shivaji's capital was at","options":["Satara","Torna","Pune","Raigad"],"answer":3,"expl":"Raigad fort served as the Maratha capital."},{"q":"The Third Battle of Panipat (1761) was fought between","options":["British and Tipu Sultan","Babur and Ibrahim Lodi","Akbar and Hemu","Ahmad Shah Abdali and the Marathas"],"answer":3,"expl":"Abdali's Afghan forces defeated the Marathas in 1761."},{"q":"The Battle of Plassey (1757) established British supremacy in","options":["Mysore","Bengal","Punjab","Awadh"],"answer":1,"expl":"Clive's victory over Siraj-ud-Daulah gave the Company Bengal."},{"q":"The Battle of Buxar was fought in","options":["1761","1772","1757","1764"],"answer":3,"expl":"The 1764 victory gave the Company the Diwani of Bengal."},{"q":"The first railway line in India (1853) ran between","options":["Kolkata and Howrah","Mumbai and Thane","Delhi and Agra","Chennai and Arcot"],"answer":1,"expl":"The 34-km Mumbai-Thane line opened on 16 April 1853."},{"q":"The Revolt of 1857 began at","options":["Meerut","Delhi","Kanpur","Lucknow"],"answer":0,"expl":"Sepoys mutinied at Meerut on 10 May 1857."},{"q":"Mangal Pandey was associated with the","options":["Kanpur siege","Meerut mutiny","Delhi capture","Barrackpore uprising"],"answer":3,"expl":"Pandey's attack on officers at Barrackpore (March 1857) sparked the revolt."},{"q":"The Indian National Congress was founded in 1885 by","options":["Gopal Krishna Gokhale","W. C. Banerjee","A. O. Hume","Dadabhai Naoroji"],"answer":2,"expl":"Retired ICS officer A. O. Hume helped found the Congress."},{"q":"The first president of the Indian National Congress was","options":["W. C. Banerjee","Dadabhai Naoroji","Surendranath Banerjee","A. O. Hume"],"answer":0,"expl":"Womesh Chandra Banerjee presided over the 1885 Bombay session."},{"q":"The partition of Bengal (1905) was ordered by","options":["Lord Curzon","Lord Minto","Lord Hardinge","Lord Chelmsford"],"answer":0,"expl":"Curzon's 1905 partition triggered the Swadeshi movement."},{"q":"The Jallianwala Bagh massacre took place in","options":["1919","1917","1921","1915"],"answer":0,"expl":"General Dyer's troops fired on the Amritsar gathering on 13 April 1919."},{"q":"Mahatma Gandhi launched the Non-Cooperation Movement in","options":["1919","1930","1922","1920"],"answer":3,"expl":"It began in August 1920 after the Jallianwala Bagh massacre and Khilafat issue."},{"q":"The Chauri Chaura incident led Gandhi to","options":["suspend the Non-Cooperation Movement","start the Quit India Movement","begin the Dandi March early","launch the Salt March"],"answer":0,"expl":"After the 1922 violence, Gandhi called off Non-Cooperation."},{"q":"The Dandi March began in the year","options":["1930","1932","1942","1928"],"answer":0,"expl":"Gandhi's Salt March ran from 12 March to 6 April 1930."},{"q":"The Quit India Movement was launched in","options":["1942","1945","1940","1939"],"answer":0,"expl":"The 'Do or Die' call came at Bombay's Gowalia Tank in August 1942."},{"q":"The Indian National Army (Azad Hind Fauj) was revived under","options":["Lala Lajpat Rai","Mohan Singh only","Rash Behari Bose only","Subhas Chandra Bose"],"answer":3,"expl":"Netaji took charge of the INA in Singapore in 1943."},{"q":"The slogan 'Jai Hind' is associated with","options":["Subhas Chandra Bose / the INA","Bhagat Singh","Nehru","Gandhi"],"answer":0,"expl":"'Jai Hind' was popularised as the INA's salutation."},{"q":"India became independent on","options":["26 January 1947","26 January 1950","15 August 1947","15 August 1948"],"answer":2,"expl":"Independence came at midnight on 15 August 1947."},{"q":"The first Governor-General of independent India was","options":["C. Rajagopalachari","Jawaharlal Nehru","Sardar Patel","Lord Mountbatten"],"answer":3,"expl":"Mountbatten served 1947-48; Rajagopalachari was the first Indian GG."},{"q":"The first Indian woman to become President of the Indian National Congress was","options":["Vijayalakshmi Pandit","Aruna Asaf Ali","Sarojini Naidu","Annie Besant"],"answer":3,"expl":"Annie Besant presided over the 1917 Calcutta session; Sarojini Naidu was the first Indian woman president (1925)."},{"q":"The Cabinet Mission came to India in","options":["1945","1944","1946","1947"],"answer":2,"expl":"The Cabinet Mission (Pethick-Lawrence, Cripps, Alexander) arrived in March 1946 to plan the transfer of power."}]},
{"id":"def-bank-geography","title":"Practice Bank — Geography (30 MCQs)","subject":"GAT (GK)","tag":"30 questions · answers with explanations","blurb":"30 exam-style geography MCQs. Pick an option on any question to see the answer and explanation instantly.","intro":"30 exam-style multiple-choice questions on Geography, written for the NDA / CDS / AFCAT / Agniveer written papers. Tap an option to check your answer — the correct answer and a short explanation appear immediately. Everything runs in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere and nothing is saved between visits.","sections":[{"h":"How to use this bank","body":"<p>Work through the questions in timed batches (aim for roughly one question per minute), then read every explanation — including the ones you got right. Patterns repeat across defence papers: the same concept keeps returning in new clothes.</p>"}],"questions":[{"q":"The largest planet in the solar system is","options":["Saturn","Jupiter","Neptune","Earth"],"answer":1,"expl":"Jupiter's mass exceeds that of all other planets combined."},{"q":"The closest planet to the Sun is","options":["Mercury","Earth","Venus","Mars"],"answer":0,"expl":"Mercury orbits nearest the Sun with a year of 88 days."},{"q":"The Earth's revolution around the Sun causes","options":["eclipses only","tides","day and night","seasons"],"answer":3,"expl":"Revolution plus axial tilt produces seasons; rotation causes day and night."},{"q":"The Tropic of Cancer passes through how many Indian states?","options":["6","12","8","10"],"answer":2,"expl":"It crosses Gujarat, Rajasthan, MP, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, West Bengal, Tripura and Mizoram."},{"q":"The longest river in India is the","options":["Ganga","Brahmaputra (in India)","Yamuna","Godavari"],"answer":0,"expl":"The Ganga (~2,525 km) is India's longest river."},{"q":"The Brahmaputra is known as Tsangpo in","options":["Myanmar","Bhutan","Tibet","Bangladesh"],"answer":2,"expl":"It rises as the Yarlung Tsangpo in Tibet before entering Arunachal as the Siang/Dihang."},{"q":"The river known as the 'Sorrow of Bihar' is the","options":["Son","Ganga","Kosi","Gandak"],"answer":2,"expl":"The Kosi's shifting course caused devastating floods in Bihar."},{"q":"The largest state of India by area is","options":["Maharashtra","Rajasthan","Uttar Pradesh","Madhya Pradesh"],"answer":1,"expl":"Rajasthan covers about 342,000 sq km."},{"q":"The smallest state of India by area is","options":["Sikkim","Manipur","Goa","Tripura"],"answer":2,"expl":"Goa (~3,700 sq km) is the smallest state."},{"q":"The southernmost point of mainland India is","options":["Kanyakumari","Indira Point","Kochi","Rameswaram"],"answer":0,"expl":"Kanyakumari (Cape Comorin) is the mainland tip; Indira Point is in the Nicobars."},{"q":"The highest peak in India is","options":["Kamet","K2 (Godwin-Austen)","Kanchenjunga","Nanda Devi"],"answer":1,"expl":"K2 (8,611 m) in PoK is India's highest peak; Kanchenjunga is the highest within undisputed territory."},{"q":"The Western Ghats are also known as","options":["Aravalli","Vindhya","Sahyadri","Nilgiri"],"answer":2,"expl":"The Sahyadri range runs along India's west coast."},{"q":"The oldest mountain range in India is the","options":["Himalaya","Western Ghats","Vindhya","Aravalli"],"answer":3,"expl":"The Aravalli is among the world's oldest fold mountain systems."},{"q":"The Thar Desert lies mainly in","options":["Gujarat","Haryana","Punjab","Rajasthan"],"answer":3,"expl":"The Thar covers western Rajasthan extending into Pakistan."},{"q":"Cherrapunji and Mawsynram receive very heavy rainfall because","options":["they lie on the windward side of the Khasi hills","they are at high altitude","they are near the sea","they face the retreating monsoon"],"answer":0,"expl":"Moist Bay of Bengal winds are forced up the Khasi hills, causing orographic rain."},{"q":"The retreating monsoon brings rain mainly to","options":["Assam","Kerala","Tamil Nadu","West Bengal"],"answer":2,"expl":"The northeast monsoon (Oct-Dec) waters the Coromandel coast, especially Tamil Nadu."},{"q":"The Indian Standard Time meridian (82.5 E) passes through","options":["Mirzapur (UP)","Allahabad city only","Nagpur","Bhopal"],"answer":0,"expl":"IST is based on 82.5 E longitude passing near Mirzapur, Uttar Pradesh."},{"q":"India's IST is ahead of GMT by","options":["5 hours","6 hours","4 hours 30 minutes","5 hours 30 minutes"],"answer":3,"expl":"IST = GMT + 5:30."},{"q":"The Palk Strait separates India from","options":["Myanmar","Sri Lanka","Bangladesh","Maldives"],"answer":1,"expl":"The Palk Strait lies between Tamil Nadu and Sri Lanka."},{"q":"The largest delta in the world is the","options":["Amazon delta","Mekong delta","Nile delta","Ganga-Brahmaputra delta"],"answer":3,"expl":"The Sundarbans delta of the Ganga-Brahmaputra is the world's largest."},{"q":"Black soil is best suited for growing","options":["cotton","tea","rice","wheat only"],"answer":0,"expl":"Regur (black) soil retains moisture, ideal for cotton."},{"q":"Laterite soil is found mainly in regions of","options":["permafrost","heavy rainfall","desert","volcanic ash only"],"answer":1,"expl":"Intense leaching in high-rainfall areas produces laterite soils."},{"q":"The Hirakud dam is built on the river","options":["Narmada","Krishna","Godavari","Mahanadi"],"answer":3,"expl":"Hirakud in Odisha dams the Mahanadi."},{"q":"The Bhakra Nangal dam is on the river","options":["Chenab","Ravi","Beas","Sutlej"],"answer":3,"expl":"Bhakra dams the Sutlej in Himachal Pradesh/Punjab."},{"q":"The Tehri dam is on the river","options":["Alaknanda","Ganga (main)","Bhagirathi","Yamuna"],"answer":2,"expl":"Tehri in Uttarakhand dams the Bhagirathi."},{"q":"Sardar Sarovar dam is on the river","options":["Tapi","Narmada","Mahi","Sabarmati"],"answer":1,"expl":"The Sardar Sarovar project dams the Narmada in Gujarat."},{"q":"The capital of Arunachal Pradesh is","options":["Shillong","Dispur","Kohima","Itanagar"],"answer":3,"expl":"Itanagar is Arunachal's capital."},{"q":"The largest freshwater lake in India is","options":["Chilika Lake","Sambhar Lake","Wular Lake","Dal Lake"],"answer":2,"expl":"Wular in J&K is India's largest freshwater lake; Chilika is brackish."},{"q":"The ozone layer is found in the","options":["thermosphere","stratosphere","troposphere","mesosphere"],"answer":1,"expl":"The ozone layer sits in the lower stratosphere, absorbing UV."},{"q":"Lines joining places of equal temperature on a map are called","options":["contours","isohyets","isobars","isotherms"],"answer":3,"expl":"Isotherms = equal temperature; isobars = equal pressure; isohyets = equal rainfall."}]},
{"id":"def-bank-polity","title":"Practice Bank — Polity (20 MCQs)","subject":"GAT (GK)","tag":"20 questions · answers with explanations","blurb":"20 exam-style polity MCQs. Pick an option on any question to see the answer and explanation instantly.","intro":"20 exam-style multiple-choice questions on Polity, written for the NDA / CDS / AFCAT / Agniveer written papers. Tap an option to check your answer — the correct answer and a short explanation appear immediately. Everything runs in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere and nothing is saved between visits.","sections":[{"h":"How to use this bank","body":"<p>Work through the questions in timed batches (aim for roughly one question per minute), then read every explanation — including the ones you got right. Patterns repeat across defence papers: the same concept keeps returning in new clothes.</p>"}],"questions":[{"q":"The Constitution of India came into force on","options":["26 November 1949","26 January 1949","26 January 1950","15 August 1947"],"answer":2,"expl":"Adopted 26 Nov 1949, it took effect on 26 Jan 1950 (Republic Day)."},{"q":"The chairman of the Drafting Committee of the Constituent Assembly was","options":["B. R. Ambedkar","Sardar Patel","Rajendra Prasad","Jawaharlal Nehru"],"answer":0,"expl":"Ambedkar chaired the committee that drafted the Constitution."},{"q":"Fundamental Rights are enshrined in Part ___ of the Constitution","options":["Part II","Part V","Part IV","Part III"],"answer":3,"expl":"Part III (Articles 12-35) guarantees Fundamental Rights."},{"q":"The Right to Equality is covered by Articles","options":["23 to 24","19 to 22","14 to 18","25 to 28"],"answer":2,"expl":"Articles 14-18 deal with equality before law and non-discrimination."},{"q":"The Right against Exploitation covers Articles","options":["29 and 30","25 and 26","19 and 20","23 and 24"],"answer":3,"expl":"Articles 23-24 prohibit trafficking, forced labour and child labour."},{"q":"Directive Principles of State Policy are in","options":["Part IV","Part III","Part V","Part VI"],"answer":0,"expl":"Part IV (Articles 36-51) holds the non-justiciable Directive Principles."},{"q":"The Preamble of the Indian Constitution was amended by the","options":["61st Amendment","44th Amendment","42nd Amendment","52nd Amendment"],"answer":2,"expl":"The 42nd Amendment (1976) added 'Socialist, Secular' and 'Integrity'."},{"q":"The minimum age to become President of India is","options":["25 years","21 years","30 years","35 years"],"answer":3,"expl":"Article 58 requires the President to be at least 35."},{"q":"The President of India is elected by","options":["an electoral college of MPs and MLAs","all citizens directly","the Lok Sabha only","the Prime Minister's cabinet"],"answer":0,"expl":"Article 54: elected MPs and MLAs form the electoral college."},{"q":"The maximum strength of the Lok Sabha is","options":["550","543","545","552"],"answer":3,"expl":"The Constitution caps Lok Sabha membership at 552."},{"q":"The Rajya Sabha is a","options":["permanent body not subject to dissolution","body dissolved every 6 years","advisory council only","body dissolved every 5 years"],"answer":0,"expl":"Rajya Sabha is continuous; one-third of members retire every two years."},{"q":"The term of a Lok Sabha member is","options":["3 years","4 years","6 years","5 years"],"answer":3,"expl":"Lok Sabha's normal term is five years unless dissolved earlier."},{"q":"The Vice-President of India is the ex-officio chairman of the","options":["Election Commission","Lok Sabha","Rajya Sabha","NITI Aayog"],"answer":2,"expl":"Article 64 makes the Vice-President the Rajya Sabha's chairman."},{"q":"The power of judicial review in India is vested in","options":["the Prime Minister","Parliament","the Supreme Court and High Courts","the President"],"answer":2,"expl":"Courts can strike down laws violating the Constitution."},{"q":"The first woman judge of the Supreme Court of India was","options":["Justice Fathima Beevi","Justice Sujata Manohar","Justice Indira Banerjee","Justice Ruma Pal"],"answer":0,"expl":"Justice M. Fathima Beevi joined the Supreme Court in 1989."},{"q":"Panchayati Raj was given constitutional status by the","options":["52nd Amendment","42nd Amendment","73rd Amendment","74th Amendment"],"answer":2,"expl":"The 73rd Amendment (1992) constitutionalised panchayats; the 74th covered municipalities."},{"q":"The Election Commission of India is a","options":["executive office","constitutional body under Article 324","judicial tribunal","statutory body"],"answer":1,"expl":"Article 324 creates the Election Commission."},{"q":"The Comptroller and Auditor General of India is appointed by the","options":["Lok Sabha Speaker","President","Chief Justice","Prime Minister"],"answer":1,"expl":"Article 148: the CAG is appointed by the President."},{"q":"Fundamental Duties were added to the Constitution by the","options":["86th Amendment","44th Amendment","24th Amendment","42nd Amendment"],"answer":3,"expl":"Part IVA (Article 51A) came via the 42nd Amendment, 1976."},{"q":"The 'basic structure' doctrine was laid down in the","options":["Kesavananda Bharati case","Golaknath case","Minerva Mills case","Maneka Gandhi case"],"answer":0,"expl":"Kesavananda Bharati (1973) held Parliament cannot alter the Constitution's basic structure."}]},
{"id":"def-bank-economy","title":"Practice Bank — Economy (14 MCQs)","subject":"GAT (GK)","tag":"14 questions · answers with explanations","blurb":"14 exam-style economy MCQs. Pick an option on any question to see the answer and explanation instantly.","intro":"14 exam-style multiple-choice questions on Economy, written for the NDA / CDS / AFCAT / Agniveer written papers. Tap an option to check your answer — the correct answer and a short explanation appear immediately. Everything runs in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere and nothing is saved between visits.","sections":[{"h":"How to use this bank","body":"<p>Work through the questions in timed batches (aim for roughly one question per minute), then read every explanation — including the ones you got right. Patterns repeat across defence papers: the same concept keeps returning in new clothes.</p>"}],"questions":[{"q":"The Reserve Bank of India was established in","options":["1935","1947","1951","1949"],"answer":0,"expl":"RBI began operations on 1 April 1935 under the RBI Act, 1934."},{"q":"The headquarters of the RBI is in","options":["Kolkata","Mumbai","Chennai","New Delhi"],"answer":1,"expl":"RBI's central office moved from Kolkata to Mumbai in 1937."},{"q":"Bank rate is the rate at which RBI","options":["lends long-term to commercial banks","lends overnight only","borrows from banks","fixes deposit rates"],"answer":0,"expl":"Bank rate is the long-term lending rate of the central bank."},{"q":"An increase in the repo rate generally","options":["makes loans cheaper","makes loans costlier","lowers all taxes","raises exports directly"],"answer":1,"expl":"Higher repo raises banks' borrowing cost, pushing up lending rates."},{"q":"Inflation is best described as a","options":["fall in prices","sustained rise in the general price level","budget surplus","rise in unemployment"],"answer":1,"expl":"Inflation means money buys less over time as prices rise broadly."},{"q":"Deflation means","options":["a sustained fall in the general price level","high growth","rising prices","stable prices"],"answer":0,"expl":"Deflation is the opposite of inflation: prices fall persistently."},{"q":"GDP stands for","options":["Gross Deposit Plan","General Domestic Price","Global Development Pact","Gross Domestic Product"],"answer":3,"expl":"GDP is the total value of goods and services produced in a country."},{"q":"The financial year in India runs from","options":["1 January to 31 December","Diwali to Diwali","1 April to 31 March","1 July to 30 June"],"answer":2,"expl":"India's fiscal year is April-March."},{"q":"The Union Budget is presented under Article","options":["114","112","110","265"],"answer":1,"expl":"Article 112 requires the Annual Financial Statement (Budget)."},{"q":"Disguised unemployment is most common in","options":["IT services","Indian agriculture","manufacturing","banking"],"answer":1,"expl":"Extra workers on family farms add nothing to output - classic disguised unemployment."},{"q":"The largest contributor to India's GDP is the","options":["services sector","agriculture sector","mining sector","manufacturing sector"],"answer":0,"expl":"Services contribute over half of India's GDP."},{"q":"NITI Aayog replaced the","options":["Election Commission","UPSC","Planning Commission","Finance Commission"],"answer":2,"expl":"NITI Aayog was formed on 1 January 2015 replacing the Planning Commission."},{"q":"GST was launched in India on","options":["15 August 2017","1 January 2018","1 April 2016","1 July 2017"],"answer":3,"expl":"Goods and Services Tax rolled out nationwide on 1 July 2017."},{"q":"A revenue deficit occurs when","options":["GDP growth turns negative","exports exceed imports","revenue expenditure exceeds revenue receipts","capital spending exceeds revenue"],"answer":2,"expl":"Revenue deficit = revenue expenditure minus revenue receipts."}]},
{"id":"def-bank-defence-gk","title":"Practice Bank — Defence GK (15 MCQs)","subject":"GAT (GK)","tag":"15 questions · answers with explanations","blurb":"15 exam-style defence gk MCQs. Pick an option on any question to see the answer and explanation instantly.","intro":"15 exam-style multiple-choice questions on Defence GK, written for the NDA / CDS / AFCAT / Agniveer written papers. Tap an option to check your answer — the correct answer and a short explanation appear immediately. Everything runs in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere and nothing is saved between visits.","sections":[{"h":"How to use this bank","body":"<p>Work through the questions in timed batches (aim for roughly one question per minute), then read every explanation — including the ones you got right. Patterns repeat across defence papers: the same concept keeps returning in new clothes.</p>"}],"questions":[{"q":"The National Defence Academy is located at","options":["Chennai","Khadakwasla, Pune","Dehradun","Ezhimala"],"answer":1,"expl":"NDA, the tri-services academy, sits at Khadakwasla near Pune."},{"q":"The Indian Military Academy is located at","options":["Dehradun","Mhow","Khadakwasla","Gaya"],"answer":0,"expl":"IMA Dehradun (estd. 1932) trains Army officers."},{"q":"The Indian Naval Academy is located at","options":["Ezhimala, Kerala","Visakhapatnam","Kochi","Mumbai"],"answer":0,"expl":"INA Ezhimala is Asia's largest naval academy."},{"q":"The Air Force Academy is located at","options":["Bidkar, Pune","Jalahalli, Bengaluru","Tambaram, Chennai","Dundigal, Hyderabad"],"answer":3,"expl":"AFA Dundigal trains Indian Air Force officers."},{"q":"India's first Chief of Defence Staff was","options":["General Manoj Mukund Naravane","Admiral Karambir Singh","General Anil Chauhan","General Bipin Rawat"],"answer":3,"expl":"Gen Bipin Rawat became the first CDS on 1 January 2020."},{"q":"The highest wartime gallantry award of India is the","options":["Param Vir Chakra","Ashoka Chakra","Maha Vir Chakra","Kirti Chakra"],"answer":0,"expl":"The PVC is India's supreme award for valour in the face of the enemy."},{"q":"The first recipient of the Param Vir Chakra was","options":["Subedar Yogendra Yadav","Major Somnath Sharma","Naik Jadunath Singh","Captain Vikram Batra"],"answer":1,"expl":"Maj Somnath Sharma earned the first PVC in the 1947 Kashmir operations."},{"q":"Indian Navy Day is observed on","options":["4 December","8 October","26 July","15 January"],"answer":0,"expl":"4 December marks the 1971 Operation Trident attack on Karachi."},{"q":"Indian Air Force Day is observed on","options":["4 December","15 January","8 October","21 February"],"answer":2,"expl":"The IAF was established on 8 October 1932."},{"q":"Indian Army Day is observed on","options":["15 January","8 October","16 December","4 December"],"answer":0,"expl":"15 January 1949 was when Gen Cariappa took over as the first Indian Army chief."},{"q":"The only two officers to hold the five-star rank of Field Marshal are","options":["Manekshaw and Arjan Singh","Chaudhuri and Manekshaw","Cariappa and Manekshaw","Cariappa and Thimayya"],"answer":2,"expl":"Field Marshal was conferred on K. M. Cariappa and Sam Manekshaw."},{"q":"The only officer of the Indian Air Force to hold five-star rank is","options":["Marshal Arjan Singh","Air Chief Marshal Pratap Lal","Air Marshal Subroto Mukerjee","Air Chief Marshal Idris Latif"],"answer":0,"expl":"Arjan Singh was made Marshal of the Air Force in 2002."},{"q":"DRDO was established in the year","options":["1974","1962","1947","1958"],"answer":3,"expl":"The Defence Research and Development Organisation was formed in 1958."},{"q":"BrahMos is a joint venture between India and","options":["Israel","USA","Russia","France"],"answer":2,"expl":"BrahMos Aerospace is an India-Russia supersonic cruise missile venture."},{"q":"INS Vikrant, commissioned in 2022, is India's","options":["first nuclear submarine","largest destroyer","first indigenous aircraft carrier","first missile frigate"],"answer":2,"expl":"INS Vikrant (2022) is the first aircraft carrier designed and built in India."}]},
{"id":"def-bank-current-affairs","title":"Practice Bank — Current Affairs (9 MCQs)","subject":"GAT (GK)","tag":"9 questions · answers with explanations","blurb":"9 exam-style current affairs MCQs. Pick an option on any question to see the answer and explanation instantly.","intro":"9 exam-style multiple-choice questions on Current Affairs, written for the NDA / CDS / AFCAT / Agniveer written papers. Tap an option to check your answer — the correct answer and a short explanation appear immediately. Everything runs in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere and nothing is saved between visits.","sections":[{"h":"How to use this bank","body":"<p>Work through the questions in timed batches (aim for roughly one question per minute), then read every explanation — including the ones you got right. Patterns repeat across defence papers: the same concept keeps returning in new clothes.</p>"}],"questions":[{"q":"Who took over as India's Chief of Defence Staff in May 2026?","options":["Admiral Krishna Swaminathan","General Dhiraj Seth","General N. S. Raja Subramani","General Anil Chauhan"],"answer":2,"expl":"Lt Gen N. S. Raja Subramani (Retd.) succeeded Gen Anil Chauhan as CDS on 31 May 2026."},{"q":"Who took over as Chief of the Army Staff in June 2026?","options":["General Upendra Dwivedi","General Dhiraj Seth","Lt Gen Pushpendra Pal Singh","General N. S. Raja Subramani"],"answer":1,"expl":"Gen Dhiraj Seth became Army chief on 30 June 2026."},{"q":"Who became Chief of Integrated Defence Staff in July 2026?","options":["Air Marshal Ashutosh Dixit","Lt Gen Johnson P. Mathew","Air Marshal Tejinder Singh","Vice Admiral Atul Kumar Jain"],"answer":2,"expl":"Air Marshal Tejinder Singh took charge as CIDS on 1 July 2026."},{"q":"Operation Sindoor (May 2025) was India's military response to","options":["a cyber attack on power grids","the Pahalgam terror attack","the Pulwama attack","a border skirmish at Galwan"],"answer":1,"expl":"After the April 2025 Pahalgam terror attack, India struck terror infrastructure in Pakistan/PoK in May 2025."},{"q":"In December 2025, the Defence Acquisition Council cleared military procurement proposals worth about","options":["Rs 7.9 lakh crore","Rs 7,900 crore","Rs 79,000 crore","Rs 79 lakh crore"],"answer":2,"expl":"The DAC meeting of 29 December 2025 approved Acceptance of Necessity for systems worth around Rs 79,000 crore."},{"q":"Under the 2026 theaterisation proposal, the planned northern theatre command is to be headquartered at","options":["Jaipur","Lucknow","Udhampur","Thiruvananthapuram"],"answer":1,"expl":"The proposal envisages a northern (China-facing) theatre at Lucknow, western at Jaipur and maritime at Thiruvananthapuram (approximate, as reported in May 2026)."},{"q":"The Agnipath scheme for recruiting Agniveers into the armed forces was launched in","options":["2022","2020","2021","2024"],"answer":0,"expl":"Agnipath was announced in June 2022, changing short-service intake across the three services."},{"q":"The Supreme Commander of the Indian Armed Forces is the","options":["Prime Minister","Defence Minister","President of India","Chief of Defence Staff"],"answer":2,"expl":"The Constitution vests supreme command of the armed forces in the President (Droupadi Murmu)."},{"q":"Air Chief Marshal A. P. Singh has been Chief of the Air Staff since","options":["January 2025","September 2024","September 2022","May 2026"],"answer":1,"expl":"ACM Amar Preet Singh took over as Air chief on 30 September 2024."}]});
